+{"parentUuid":"ea4efc4a-b9e2-4092-aaa2-b669f3067567","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"9b161755-a84f-4b72-81a7-8fba1824e9e4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SDwKrT2ePrCjRWDvVPp7kg","type":"tool_result","content":"96c96\n< The Goal body ends with the run's constraints, one heading per line, so a run and a prompt generator can find them:\n---\n> The Goal body ends with the run's constraints block, so a run and `hyp prompt` can find it:\n103a104,105\n> The block runs from the first line that starts with `May change:`, `Read only:` or `Never:` to the end of the body. It holds each of the three headings at least once, and every non-empty line in it starts with one of them, so write one constraint per line and put no other text after the block. A line of spaces, a tab or a lone carriage return is not empty: leave a blank line truly empty, and write the body with `\\n` line ends. A heading may repeat, such as one `Never:` line per act. `hyp add goal` warns when a body has a heading but no valid block, and `hyp prompt` refuses that Goal.\n> \n108,112c110\n< Write the `/goal` prompt for hyp-goal-run, under 4000 characters: the tree, the runner's actor, the Goal id, the stop clauses, and the Goal body's `May change:`, `Read only:` and `Never:` lines copied as they are. Put general practice in the skill, not the prompt. For a build Goal it also says:\n< - the branch, and that the run opens a PR and never merges;\n< - the write scope covers every text the change makes wrong, the design record included;\n< - one review round per candidate commit, by a fresh subagent given the lines, the code and the keys already settled with the owner's words;\n< - sort each finding: a case of a line is a limit; a question about a line's meaning is a reading; a defect in text the run may edit is a fix commit; anything else is an out-of-Goal finding, named in the stop checkpoint and never holding the select.\n---\n> hyp prints the `/goal` prompt for hyp-goal-run from the log. Choose a new runner actor, then run:\n114c112,114\n< A prompt that states tree state (an accept, a select) is written from the merged log, or names the PR it waits for.\n---\n> ```text\n> hyp prompt <G-id> --actor <runner-actor> [--branch <branch>] [--pr <N>] [--experiments <N>]\n> ```\n115a116,117\n> It prints the store's path, the actor, the Goal id, the skill, the stop clauses (the runner owes nothing, or N Results under the actor, 5 by default) and the Goal body's constraints block, line for line; `--branch` and `--pr` add a clause each after the block. Give the owner its output as it is, and do not add to it. The prompt carries no owner answer: OWED lists each answer and each returned limit the runner applies. The practice of a run, a build Goal's review round included, is in hyp-goal-run. Run `hyp prompt` on the log the run starts from: the prompt names the Goal by its id, so the Goal's add must be in that log, merged or on the branch the prompt names. If `hyp prompt` refuses (exit 1), the Goal body has no valid constraints block; a Goal is never edited, so tell the owner, who adds a new Goal with a valid block. Exit 2 is a bad argument, such as a `--branch` with a space; exit 3 means no repository holds the store, so run it from the store inside its repository.\n> \ntotal 0\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 8 datguy  staff  256 Nov 12 21:06 .\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 4 datguy  staff  128 Nov 12 21:06 ..\ndrwxr-x---@ 9 datguy  staff  288 Nov 12 21:06 ak-skill-creator\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 3 datguy  staff   96 Nov 12 21:06 hyp-goal-run\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 3 datguy  staff   96 Nov 12 21:06 hyp-owner-answer\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 3 datguy  staff   96 Nov 12 21:06 hyp-owner-grill\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 4 datguy  staff  128 Nov 12 21:06 hyp-prototype\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 4 datguy  staff  128 Nov 12 21:06 hyp-verification\npackage cli\n\nimport (\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\ninternal/cli:\nanswer_record_test.go\nclaim_test.go\ncli.go\ncontain_test.go\ncross_goal_moves_test.go\nevents_test.go\nexitcode_test.go\nexitcod