s Result gave a verdict on: a limit is a case the Goal covers that no check ran. If a test within the Goal's lines can run the case on every candidate, add an Experiment with `--aims` at it, run it, and record `--closes` on the Result that ran the case. A close records that the case ran, whatever the verdicts: a candidate that fails the case is contradicted and the limit is still closed, so never write a rule that closes it only on a pass. A close covers every case the limit's text names; `hyp record` prints that text when it closes. Record each case you did not run as a new `--limit` on the closing Result, and refer that new key when its case is void or outside the Goal's lines. A claim that a case is void, out of scope or covered elsewhere is a limit and a refer, never a sentence in a body or an observation. When that claim rests on your reading of a Goal line, put the reading in the refer's text. Write one case per `--limit`. If no such test can, or the case is void for every candidate, refer it with your reason: `hyp checkpoint --refers \"<key>=<why no test within the Goal can close it, or why the case is void>\" --why \"...\"`; hyp refuses a refer with no reason. When the owner returns a referred limit, it is an ordinary `limit` line again.\n5. End with the candidates left under each Question: none, one, or several, by alias.\n6. Select only when OWED lists `select` for that Question: `hyp select <Q-id> <H-id> --why \"...\"`. Never confirm a select. Do not change the Goal or add new structures unless the owner asks.\n\n## Goal lines\n\n- The Goal's lines are limits keyed on the Goal, `G-<id>#<n>`; `hyp status` prints them under LINES. A Goal's lines come with its add and no later event adds one. If the Goal's body states lines and none is typed, they cannot be added to this Goal: tell the owner, who may add a new Goal with them (`hyp add goal … --line \"<line>\"`). Do not write them for the owner.\n- A line is owed as a `limit` on each top-level Question with a