+{"parentUuid":"cc1bc223-fade-4611-97b4-35af58884759","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"8d72d7f5-1fda-4169-90c7-b293556da6ca","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01836S9Q58FQ9f2utCr8DBKZ","type":"tool_result","content":"suggested: none (Q-H4QXXH (q-where) owes the runner first: limit G-VRG6SA#1)\ncheck: 0 errors, 0 warnings\n136:CLI error text is `refused: <reason>` on stderr, with exit code 1. A help request (`-h` or `--help` on any command, and `help` at the top level and on a group command) prints usage on stdout and exits 0 at every level, while a missing or unknown subcommand exits 2.\n233:- `complete` when the Goal has at least one live DQ as a child and every live DQ whose parent is the Goal is `answered`; a Goal with no live top-level DQ is `in_progress`, because nothing has been asked yet. It reads no line: a line of the Goal is owed on each top-level question as a `limit` item (§8.1) and the select waits on it, and the owner's \"met\" is a complete Goal with no line under OWED. hyp stores no word for it. Sub-questions are covered through the `answered` rule of §7.4, so sub-questions under rejected rival hypotheses never block the Goal. (This clarifies the advice's \"nothing stale\" clause: stale on the selected path already prevents `answered`.)\n344:Lines **[added]**: a line of the Goal (§4 lines) is a limit with the Goal for its Result. It has a hand and closers as a limit does, and is neither open nor closed: whether its case ran is per candidate, its coverage. `Open()` of a line means only that its hand is not an accept. **Coverage**: a Result covers a Hypothesis when it has a verdict on it, or when one of the Result's run subjects holds the Hypothesis's whole subject (§4 subject; `Projection.Covers`). Coverage says the bytes ran, not that the claim held. A line is **covered** on a question when a closer of the line covers each of its candidates, and **uncovered** there otherwise. An unaccepted, unreferred line is owed on each live top-level question where it is uncovered (`Projection.Uncovered`), so a new candidate is owed the line again. Coverage is not a state of the move table: it changes when a candidate is added or falls, with no move on the key, and no move reads it. `hyp show G-…#n` prints the line's text, its hand when it has one (`hand: accepted by o`), each cite with what it meant and, when that differs, what it would mean now (`cites H-…: realised, now unsettled`), each top-level question's candidates with `covered` or `uncovered: H-…`, each closer with what it ran on (`ran in R-…: ran on H-… (alias): supports; ran on H-…`), the aiming Experiments and the history. A verdict is per Hypothesis, never per line: a Result that closes several lines and contradicts H says the cases ran and H failed, not which line, so nothing prints `fails <line>`. `hyp status` prints a LINES section under the GOAL line, one line each with its facts joined by `; `, each only when it has one: its hand, `covered on Q-…, Q-…` and `uncovered on Q-…: H-…` for each live top-level question with a candidate, and `ran in R-…`; a line with none prints `no candidate`. Neither `open` nor `closed` prints for a line. The section also prints and the mark `(no one closer covers H-… and H-… together)` when the Hypotheses selected on two or more top-level questions were never covered by one closer (`Projection.NotTogether`); a claim that depends on its host is covered only together with it. No item and no status reads the mark. `hyp show <Goal>` lists the lines, and `hyp show <H>` prints a tied subject.\n350:`Projection.Owed()` derives the ledger: each item of open work on a question, with who owes it. An open or reopened question owes every kind of item; an answered one owes only the `limit`, `reading`, `referred` and `premise` items, so evidence that lands after the answer is owed and not only listed. Nothing stores the ledger, and owing an item never changes a status: the gate is at the write (§5.1), not in the derivation. Settling one is always an explicit event; a planned test is intent, not an outcome, so an Experiment's `aims` never settles a limit or a reading, and settling an item never selects, answers a question or meets the Goal. OWED is per Goal: an item belongs to the Goal its question's parents lead to, a line is owed only on the top-level questions of its own Goal, and coverage of a line reads only those questions' candidates. `hyp status G-…` prints one Goal: its GOAL, LINES, FRONTIER, STALE and OWED, then AWAITING OWNER, OPEN LIMITS, RECENT, CHECKPOINT, QUESTIONABLE and UNCONTAINED, each reading only that Goal's items, so each equals what the Goal's own events give in a store of their own; REJECTED counts the store's rejections. An event belongs to a Goal when its node leads to it, or, for an answer, which is on no node, when one of its keys does. CHECKPOINT is the Goal's latest runner checkpoint (`LastCheckpoint`); an old owner checkpoint is history and not one. `hyp status` with no Goal prints that view of the one Goal in a one-Goal store, as it always did. In a store with many Goals it prints a GOALS table, one row per Goal in creation order with its state and counts, `runner owes N` or `runner owes nothing`, and its last checkpoint; then one AWAITING OWNER list, grouped by Goal, each Goal with an item under its own heading; then REJECTED. Items, per question in creation order:\n358:| `limit` | runner | an open limit under a question that has a candidate, whichever Hypotheses its Result judged, unless its latest hand is a refer; or, on a live top-level question, an open line of its Goal that is not referred and that a candidate has no closer covering | a Result that `closes` it (for a line, one that covers each candidate), or a `refers` |\n359:| `referred` | owner | an open limit's latest hand is a refer, whichever Hypotheses its Result judged; a referred line is owed on each live top-level question; `hyp status` prints `limit referred, accept or return` | an owner checkpoint that `accepts` or `returns` it |\n369:2. `waits`: something must happen before a select or a choice. The turn lists every such thing, in this order: runner work (any runner item on Q, the first one named); an open reading on Q's Results with a reach (§4 readings); a referred limit under Q, or a referred line when Q is top-level; a premise (below); a sub-question not answered of the current select's Hypothesis when Q reopened `awaiting_subquestions`, or else of the lone candidate (ADR 0014). A choice between two or more candidates does not wait for sub-questions.\n404:An accept answers a refer, except the history row: an old owner confirm's `accepts` applied with no refer, and replays as it was written. A new accept needs a refer, so a waive is always two steps, the runner's refer and the owner's accept, and after a return a new accept needs a new refer. Separation of duties compares the answer with the latest refer, never with the author of the key's Result or of the Goal, so an owner answers a refer of their own line. A refer of an accepted limit is refused because only the owner reopens one, and a refer of a closed one because its case ran: no test is left to refer. A line is never closed, so a line that ran on some candidates can still be referred. A move never crosses Goals: a refer, close, aim or apply whose key leads to another Goal is refused (§5 rule 32), so an item is moved only by its own Goal's runner and by the owner, and OWED, which is per Goal, names the hand the tables store. They disagree in two named cases, each accept-able by the table and owed by nobody: a referred limit on a deferred or withdrawn question, and a referred line on a Goal with no live top-level question. Within one event the moves apply in the order refers, returns, accepts. A refer and an answer's moves are never in one event: owner and runner fields never mix (§5 rule 30), and one answer may move keys of several Goals.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"61456