rompt.\n- `hyp prompt` prints, in order: the store path, the actor, the Goal id, the skill name hyp-goal-run, stop clauses (the runner owes nothing under that Goal's OWED; or N Results recorded under the actor, N from --experiments, default 5), then the block line for line, then one clause each for --branch and --pr. Same log gives identical bytes. In a store with several Goals it never includes another Goal's items.\n- Exit codes: bad argument such as --branch with whitespace = 2; no repository around the store = 3; unknown Goal id = 1.\n- Goals are never edited after they are added.\n\nWalk each rule through scenarios the designers did not draw: two Goals in one store where one body contains the other Goal's id or a line beginning with a heading word inside prose; a merged log from two actors where the same Goal appears from both branches; a base/branch that moves mid-run; a Goal whose body uses CRLF line ends; a Goal added by a log that predates the block (for example investigations/checkpoint-store G-D1CH1R); an --actor equal to the owner's actor; an N-Results count when a Result is retracted or the actor's Results come from a merged log; --experiments 0 or negative. For each, say whether the rules give a single defined behaviour, a conflict, or a gap, and cite the file and line of the code or spec that shows it. Report only facts you verified in the source, each with file:line, and keep the report under 500 words."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
+{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"1fd7e156-53e8-43a6-92ae-cc9f5bba5a3f","hash":"bcfcc1bfba4898debebd2a765f4614fdcd2932236085aaa63a36a2c9b0e4fed2","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013av9jq2bTBjg8rLGj4vD4c","type":"tool_result","content":"DesignQuestion Q-PC04ED\nalias: q-commit\ntitle: Which hyp commit implements the shared-store design?\npath: G-D1CH1R > Q-PC04ED\nstatus: answered\ncandidates: H-P4YGE2\nselected: H-P4YGE2 — OWED owes this select: R-RRYBPH on 4047c01 (branch feat/one-subject-per-run) supports H-P4YGE2 under E-GAT8N4's rule and closed R-9SMRB2#4 and G-D1CH1R#1 to #8; #9 is accepted by the owner.\ndesign: Commit 4047c01 on branch feat/one-subject-per-run, from 03e9e3d. Applies the owner's return of R-9SMRB2#4: ADR 0020 writes a run subject as {base, patch, files}, as the code, replay-spec and glossary do. The code (cmd/, internal/) is unchanged since b80d95d (git diff b80d95d 4047c01 -- cmd internal is empty), whose removal checks R-P1M8A8 logged.\nlimits:\n  R-0HABWN#1 closed by R-CX1G1C (H-P65RW9 (one-subject-per-arm) supports) — F1: a sandbox record with two --patch Arms at base B is kept as one run subject {B, [pa, pb]}, so a Hypothesis tied to B plus Arm a's patch is never covered though that Arm ran at B; a subject has at most one patch.\n  R-0HABWN#2 accepted by owner-dat — F2: line 3's new tests fail to build on 9429fc1, so neither half fails on the commit before its change with a message naming its rule (line 9's case for line 3).\n  R-0HABWN#3 closed by R-CX1G1C (H-P65RW9 (one-subject-per-arm) supports) — F3: no test runs rule 26 on a base: a bare-B subject re-tied after a run at B plus a patch must be admitted, and refused with 'subject ran in' after a run at exactly B.\n  R-0HABWN#4 closed by R-CX1G1C (H-P65RW9 (one-subject-per-arm) supports) — F4: a Hypothesis tied with --base B and two paths of the same bytes is never held, because run files are unique by sha256 and subject files unique by path.\n  R-0HABWN#5 closed by R-CX1G1C (H-P65RW9 (one-subject-per-arm) supports) — F5: hyp subject --base exits 2 when git is not on PATH; a missing tool is an environment problem, exit 3.\n  R-0HABWN#6 closed by R-CX1G1C (H-P65RW9 (one-subject-per-arm) supports) — F6: replay admits a Result whose subjects are not empty while its evidence is empty, though subjects are copied only from cited records.\n  R-0HABWN#7 closed by R-7XVMDC (H-W35076 supports) — Q: a sandbox run at B with an --inputs file never covers a Hypothesis whose subject is bare B, because a based subject is held only by a run given exactly its files.\n  R-9SMRB2#1 closed by R-Q9WTZB (H-JA5S86 contradicts), R-KBW141 (H-AX7M2K supports) — F1: a Hypothesis tied to B plus arm.patch is covered by a run at bare B with no Arm whose --inputs file has the patch's bytes, though the patch never changed the code.\n  R-9SMRB2#2 closed by R-Q9WTZB (H-JA5S86 contradicts), R-KBW141 (H-AX7M2K supports) — F2: a Hypothesis tied to B plus an input file I is held by a run at B whose patch is I, because a Hypothesis's subject does not say which of its files is the patch.\n  R-9SMRB2#3 closed by R-Q9WTZB (H-JA5S86 contradicts), R-KBW141 (H-AX7M2K supports) — F3: replay-spec rule 27 still names 'a subject with no base and no files' and not the new 'no base, no patch and no files' or 'patch ... is not a sha256' rejections.\n  R-9SMRB2#4 closed by R-RRYBPH (H-P4YGE2 supports) — F4: ADR 0020 still writes a run subject as {base, files} with no patch field, while the code, replay-spec and glossary write {base, patch, files}.\n  R-9SMRB2#5 accepted by owner-dat — F5: a Result written by a 7257753 or 3ec4979 binary keeps an Arm's patch inside files, so 0cda2f8 replays it as a run at B given input P, which covers bare B.\n  R-9SMRB2#6 closed by R-Q9WTZB (H-JA5S86 contradicts), R-KBW141 (H-AX7M2K supports) — F6: no test runs rule 26 after the owner's case: once a run at B given inputs holds a bare-B Hypothesis, a new tie of that Hypothesis must be refused with 'subject ran in'.\n  R-9SMRB2#7 accepted by owner-dat — Q: a sandbox with Arms keeps only patched subjects, so a bare-B control its Launch builds gives no bare-B subject, and a bare-B Hypothesis stays uncovered by it.\n  R-3EN4FB#1 closed by R-P1M8A8 (H-71PQR7 supports) — G1: a sandbox.json Arm whose sha256 is not lowercase hex is kept as a run at bare B with no patch, so that patched run covers a Hypothesis whose subject is bare B.\n  R-3EN4FB#2 closed by R-P1M8A8 (H-71PQR7 supports) — G2: hyp subject H arm.patch --base B --patch arm.patch is admitted, with one path as both the patch and an input, so a run at B plus that Arm never covers H.\n  R-3EN4FB#3 closed by R-P1M8A8 (H-71PQR7 supports) — G3: replay-spec rule 28 says a records path named twice is refused with 'bad subject: <path> listed twice', while the code says 'bad records: <path> named twice'.\n  R-3EN4FB#4 closed by R-P1M8A8 (H-71PQR7 supports) — G4: replay-spec section 6 writes the projection's Subjects as a map to []SubjectFile, while the code holds a HypothesisSubject, so the spec cannot show a subject's base or patch.\nexperiments:\n  E-QA0X14 tests H-CF1YR3\n    judged: H-CF1YR3=contradicts\n{\"format\":1,\"events\":[{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"01M74Y0D72WN8RS0X4R0D1CH1R\",\"ts\":\"2026-11-11T09:11:16.450904Z\",\"actor\":\"claude-g1-1\",\"type\":\"node.add\",\"node\":\"01M74Y0D72WN8RS0X4R0D1CH1R\",\"payload\":{\"kind\":\"Goal\",\"parent\":\"\",\"title\":\"hyp implements the shared store: one store holds many Goals\",\"body\":\"Builds the design recorded in checkpoint-store-design:G-73R704 (selects on checkpoint-store-design-D906CD, checkpoint-store-design-HC4V9T, checkpoint-store-design-H8PJ20, checkpoint-store-design-R21S8R). Every decision is selected there with session e6a35a24 as evidence, and ADR 0020 cites those Results. The candidate is a hyp commit; go test on its base and on the candidate is the instrument. Two-Goal stores land first, so lines 2, 6 and 7 are red on that commit's parent.\