s, which is a choice of example rather than a split. The stacking example names a generic `harness@1`.\n- [sandbox-spec.md](sandbox-spec.md) §5.2 records choices made in design trees that are core-scope, so that is ordinary provenance.\n- [how-hyp-improves-itself.md](how-hyp-improves-itself.md), \"Guard the loop while it runs, not before\", describes the tripwire that stops a run; the session-replay recipe whose `tripwire.py` built it stays in the private development history.\n\nSkill text reaches replayed runs, so moving a passage out of the skills is its own change, a build Goal like any change to `skills/`. This page is the owner decision such a change can cite.\n3:The words hyp uses for its tree, its recipes and its sandboxes. The specs ([replay-spec.md](replay-spec.md), [sandbox-spec.md](sandbox-spec.md)) and the recipe format define the rules; this file only fixes which word means what, links the rule's section instead of restating it, and names the Go type that owns each concept where one exists. It also names the two scopes of this repository, core and self-review, and the test that puts an item in one; [core-and-self-review.md](core-and-self-review.md) maps the repository onto them.\n22:The name a write records as its author, from `--actor` or `HYP_ACTOR` ([spec §1](replay-spec.md#1-names-and-locations)). Each actor appends to its own events file, so logs from several worktrees merge ([spec §6](replay-spec.md#6-merge-order)). An actor is not a role: owner is the role an owner checkpoint names (the verifier role is retired, ADR 0015).\n46:A Goal whose candidate is a commit: building is a Goal like any other. A line asserts one property of the candidate, decided by its own tests or one pinned instrument: `[P]` pins what a valid input produces, and `[N]` pins that a forbidden state is refused. Each commit is a Hypothesis whose subject is the base plus that commit's change, and a fix is a new Hypothesis that supersedes it. Each Experiment's verdict rule asks for red on base and green on the candidate; the base of the red run's subject is where the build starts, and the Goal records no start commit. A finding of a run's review that is a case of a line is a limit; one that is not is an out-of-Goal finding. A change to a skill is a build Goal too (ADR 0021).\n144:Retired by ADR 0019. It was the `confirm` event in which a role judged a select `sufficient` or `insufficient`; the last use was the owner's confirm of a scope-change select. `hyp confirm` exits 2. An old owner confirm replays only the limits it accepted ([spec §4](replay-spec.md#4-event-types-closed-set)), and an old verifier confirm applies nothing. The owner still \"confirms\" in plain speech when they choose or accept.\n145:_Avoid_: approve, review; verdict (alone: a confirm's answer was a confirm verdict)\n154:**Verifier**:\n155:Retired by ADR 0015. It was the role that confirmed every select, independently of the actor who made it. Old verifier confirms replay as history and apply nothing, and `hyp confirm` is retired (ADR 0019). Any actor may still review: a defect is recorded as a Result, a retract or a limit. In code: `event.RoleVerifier`, read only from old logs.\n156:_Avoid_: reviewer, checker (as a role)\n179:A finding, often from a run's review, that is a case of none of the Goal's lines and asks no question about what a line means. It is not a limit, since a limit is a case the Goal covers, so it gets no tree event, no refer and no accept, and it never holds the select. The runner names it in its stop checkpoint's summary and its last message as a possible new Goal or issue, and the owner decides at global scope whether to add one. A defect in text the run changed and may edit is not one: the run fixes it with a commit.\n258:**verifier-loop**:\n259:Retired with the verifier role (ADR 0015). It was a select that collected two or more insufficient verifier verdicts in one chain.\n438:Every concept hyp has, and everything a user who installs hyp into another repository runs, loads or reads there. A concept stays core when self-review is what tests it.\n441:**Self-review**:\n442:The recipes this repository keeps to test hyp, whatever repository the runner works on, with the inputs only they use. Investigating hyp is an activity, not a scope: a tree about hyp takes the scope of its question, and a tree about another project is a self-review input.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sandboxViolations":[],"sandboxed":true,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"659e71f6-59a3-4878-848b-cfc5258a02a4","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"rule","ruleSource":"cliArg"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-cli","cwd":"/Users/datguy/.hyp-sandbox/g146-base-b-1/wt","sessionId":"622e2859-9f06-412d-9cd1-74a2fa1e7a14","version":"2.1.322","gitBranch":"work"}
+{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"622e2859-9f06-412d-9cd1-74a2fa1e7a14","hash":"cd9366a45495edf6b8521ad14b6a2d018c8ec223a1ff26c94a9edff252a5a253","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017HmTVGNnzatFU32MuKXXxo","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/datguy/.hyp-sandbox/g146-base-b-1/wt && ls investigations investigations/goal-prompt 2>&1; cat skills/hyp-goal-run/SKILL.md; grep -rn \"experiments\\|Experiments\" internal/prompt* cmd/hyp/*prompt* 2>/dev/null | head -30"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0179Nqx7zFmKzbjNx9uD5rq6","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/datguy/.hyp-sandbox/g146-base-b-1/wt && cat docs/core-and-self-review.md | head -80; grep -n -i \"verifier\\|review\" docs/glossary.md | head -20"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
+{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"622e2859-9f06-412d-9cd1-74a2fa1e7a14","hash":"9696710878f2943611d463ddf414db0383010987a2e2f5202d7c678d2cc393e6","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017HmTVGNnzatFU32MuKXXxo","type":"tool_result","content":"investigations:\ncheckpoint-store\ncheckpoint-store-design\ngoal-prompt\nid-citation\nrecipe-base\n\ninvestigations/goal-prompt:\nevals\n---\nname: hyp-goal-run\ndescription: \"Run a hyp tree under an owner's Goal and met rule as an unattended /goal, for a discovery Goal or a build Goal: act only on what OWED says the runner owes, test before asking, write readings the owner can answer, reassess the targets under the owner's answers, and stop with a report the owner can read from hyp status. Use when a /goal prompt names this skill, such as the one hyp prompt prints.\"\n---\n\nThe `/goal` prompt names the tree, your actor, the Goal, the stop and the Goal body's constraints; `hyp prompt <goal-id> --actor <actor>` prints it from the log. This skill says how to work until that stop. Use it with the hyp-prototype skill, which says how to add Questions, Hypotheses, Experiments and Results, and the hyp-verification skill, which says how to run an Experiment.\n\n## Start\n\n- Read the Goal first with `hyp show <goal-id>`, including its checkpoints, because `hyp status` does not print its met rule.\n- Everything you need is in the tree. Do not read session logs, reports or memory about earlier runs.\n- The Goal's body states the run's constraints under three headings: `May change:` (what you may write, and how), `Read only:` (what you may read but never change) and `Never:` (acts you never take). Obey them. A `/goal` prompt may narrow them, never widen them. If a step needs something they forbid, refer it with a reason or record a reading, and stop.\n- A store may hold many Goals. Then `hyp status` prints only a GOALS table and one Awaiting owner list, so run `hyp status <goal-id>` wherever this skill says `hyp status`: OWED, LINES, the stop line and the last checkpoint are per Goal. Name the Goal with `--goal <goal-id>` on `hyp checkpoint`.\n- Print `hyp status` at the end of every turn.\n\n## Work\n\nAct on what OWED in `hyp status` says the runner owes, and on nothing else. Plan only the next Experiment.\n\n- Use the Goal's must-fail cases as known-answer flaw cases, in the owner's terms.\n- The recipe's checks may exist before the verdict rule, because `hyp add exp` pins them. Write and run no candidate until the rule is recorded on the Experiment.\n- Try a new or changed check only on its cases' stand-ins, never on a candidate, until the rule is recorded. If a candidate shows a check is wrong anyway, do not just fix the check: write the case that shows it, in the Goal's terms with the outcome it must give, in a new version, and name that case in the Experiment body. A sentence that discloses a trial run pins nothing.\n- `hyp record` and `hyp check` warn when a Result's text disagrees with its cited files: a verdict that a cited `verdict:` line contradicts, or a commit or hash in the observation or a limit that no cited file holds. Fix each one with a new Result that cites the file; a Result cannot change after it is recorded.\n\n## Build Goals\n\nA build Goal's candidate is a commit. Its practice:\n\n- Each commit you test is one Hypothesis under the Goal's question, with the branch and the commit in its title. Tie its subject before the run with `hyp subject <H> --base <commit>`. A fix is a new commit and a new Hypothesis that supersedes the old one (`hyp supersede <new> --old <old>`).\n- Write the tree with the installed hyp. Compile the candidate to a separate path, and never replace an installed binary.\n- Pin the gate as a recipe when the repository keeps one, so a script decides the verdict. When none does, the Experiment on a commit uses `--recipe none --rubric reading`: its body states the gate as the rule, and the Result cites the gate's logs.\n- Before you run anything, write each Experiment's verdict rule in its body: the Goal's lines it aims at, red on the base and green on the candidate for each new test, a removal check for each refusal (remove the refusal and show which test fails), and `go vet ./...` and `go test ./... -count=1`, or the repository's own gate. For skill text, run the original and the candidate skills on the same eval cases.\n- Keep the logs under `.tree/evidence/<E-id>/` and cite them in the Result.\n- Before the stop, ask a fresh subagent for one review of each candidate commit's diff against the design and the Goal's lines. Give it the code, the lines and the keys already settled, with the owner's words; do not give it your conclusion. Ask it to label each finding with the line it is a case of, or none.\n- Sort each finding: a case of a line is a `--limit` you close or, only if void, refer; a question about a line's meaning is a reading; a defect in text you may edit is a fix commit; anything else is an out-of-Goal finding, named in the stop checkpoint, and it never holds the select.\n- The run may push its branch and open or update one PR. It never merges.\n\n## Before a trade-off\n\nBefore a Question goes to the owner as a trade-off, add one structure that is not among the candidates, and the cheapest variant of each contradicted rival that would hold. Run them through a new version of the pinned recipe and record the Result. If a new structure holds every check, say in a checkpoint that the rule does not decide this Question.\n\n## Readings\n\n- When a verdict rests on your reading of a Goal line, write the other reading as a strict check: a separate named command in the recipe, with its own known answers, that sets no verdict. Say so in the Experiment's verdict rule, and run it on the same candidates. Record the reading with `hyp record --reading`, naming the check and which candidates fail it. If the recipe cannot test the other reading, say that in the reading.\n- When a verdict rests on the word of a reading that is still open under AWAITING OWNER, or on an owner answer another Result already reads, record the same reading on that Result too, word for word, with the same `--reading-of` key if it has one: a reading holds only its own Result's Question, and hyp prints identical readings as one question and warns when the owner answers only one. Record a copy only on a Result that judged a Hypothesis whose verdict differs from the strict check's outcome for it: a `supports` the check fails, or a `contradicts` the check passes. A Result whose judged Hypotheses the check gives the same outcome as the verdict gets no copy. The owner answers every copy in one answer, because `--answer` is repeatable.\n- Before you record a reading, look for a test that can decide it. If a test fits the Goal's lines, write it, run it under the verdict rule, and record no reading. If a test needs a new Goal line, name the test, the fact it measures and the line in the reading, and ask the owner whether to run it. Ask the owner to choose a meaning only when no test can decide, and say why none can.\n- Write each reading in ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English: short sentences, active verbs, one meaning for each word, and the Goal's words for things. Use this form:\n\n  ```text\n  <Goal line>: \"<the words>\" has two meanings. Meaning 1: <...>. Meaning 2: <...>. This run used meaning <n>. <Check> tests meaning <n>; <candidates> fail it. Question: <one question the owner can answer in their own words>?\n  ```\n\n  Give one small example of each meaning, in the Goal's terms. If the line does not have two meanings, state the case it does not clearly cover, then the question. A reading holds at most 600 characters.\n\n## Owner answers\n\n- When the owner answers a reading, record it with their exact words, `hyp answer --answer \"<R-id>#r<n>=<their words>\" --actor <owner-actor>`, and treat their answer as the meaning of that line. If the owner's words answer a reading but do not name it, ask which reading before you write; an answer must name its key. Do not leave a question only in chat.\n- If the owner's answer itself has two meanings, record a reading of that answer with `hyp record --reading-of <R-id>#r<n>=\"<the form above>\"`. It narrows the answer and never reopens it; the answer stays in force.\n- Only the owner changes an answer. When they give new words for a reading they already answered, record them with the same `hyp answer --answer \"<R-id>#r<n>=<their new words>\"`; hyp derives that it is a revision. The earlier answer and the Results that applied it stay. Apply the new answer with a Result that `--applies` it, and test a successor (`hyp supersede`) of any Hypothesis the old answer contradicted.\n- A limit the owner accepts is recorded with `hyp answer --accept \"<key>=<their words>\" --actor <owner-actor>`, and a return with `--returns`. hyp admits an accept only of a limit you referred, and never under your actor.\n\n## Reassess\n\nApply each answered reading that `hyp status` lists under OWED as `apply answer`, and act on each limit it lists.\n\n1. For each answer, write one line: the owner's words, the check, and the candidates that fail it. If the owner is in this session, show the lines and wait for their yes. If not, write the lines in the Experiment's body.\n2. For each Question, add one Experiment that names each answer it applies with `--aims`, and fix how its verdicts are decided before you run it: a rubric from `rubrics/` (`--rubric <name>@<version>`, a new rubric version when the answer changes the rule) or `--rubric reading` with the rule in its body. A recipe measures and never prints a verdict. To reassess records an earlier run kept, pin them with `--records .tree/evidence/<E-id>` and `--recipe none`. If an outcome is already known, say so in the rule. Give a verdict on every target, `inconclusive` when the run could not decide. Record its Result with `--applies` for each answer it applied. Old Results stay as they are.\n3. If applying an answer raises a new question about what a Goal line or the owner's answer means, record it with `hyp record --reading` on that Result. A Result cannot change after it is recorded, so if it is already recorded, record a new Result under the same Experiment.\n4. Act on each `limit` line under OWED, whichever Hypothesis its Result gave a verdict on: a limit is a case the Goal covers that no check ran. If a test within the Goal's lines can run the case on every candidate, add an Experiment with `--aims` at it, run it, and record `--closes` on the Result that ran the case. A close records that the case ran, whatever the verdicts: a candidate that fails the case is contradicted and the limit is still closed, so never write a rule that closes it only on a pass. A close covers every case the limit's text names; `hyp record` prints that text when it closes. Record each case you did not run as a new `--limit` on the closing Result, and refer that new key when its case is void or outside the Goal's lines. A claim that a case is void, out of scope or covered elsewhere is a limit and a refer, never a sentence in a body or an observation. When that claim rests on your reading of a Goal line, put the reading in the refer's text. Write one case per `--limit`. If no such test can, or the case is void for every candidate, refer it with your reason: `hyp checkpoint --refers \"<key>=<why no test within the Goal can close it, or why the case is void>\" --why \"...\"`; hyp refuses a refer with no reason. When the owner returns a referred limit, it is an ordinary `limit` line again.\n5. End with the candidates left under each Question: none, one, or several, by alias.\n6. Select only when OWED lists `select` for that Question: `hyp select <Q-id> <H-id> --why \"...\"`. Never confirm a select. Do not change the Goal or add new structures unless the owner asks.\n\n## Goal lines\n\n- The Goal's lines are limits keyed on the Goal, `G-<id>#<n>`; `hyp status` prints them under LINES. A Goal's lines come with its add and no later event adds one. If the Goal's body states lines and none is typed, they cannot be added to this Goal: tell the owner, who may add a new Goal with them (`hyp add goal … --line \"<line>\"`). Do not write them for the owner.\n- A line is owed as a `limit` on each top-level Question with a candidate no closer covers. Run its case like any limit: aim at it with `--aims G-<id>#<n>`, and record `--closes G-<id>#<n>` on each Result that ran the case. A Result covers a candidate it gave a verdict on, or one whose whole subject (base, patch and files) one of the Result's cited runs holds, so tie each candidate before the run: `hyp subject <H> [PATH…] --base <commit> [--patch FILE]`.\n- A line is never edited. What a line means is a reading. A line no test within the Goal can run is a refer, and the owner accepts or returns it. A new line or a new purpose is a new Goal, so a new tree: tell the owner, and do not write it.\n\n## Stop\n\nStop on the clauses the `/goal` prompt names. Stopping is not success.\n\n1. Before your last message, record a runner checkpoint, so the fork does not live only in chat; the owner reads `hyp status`, which prints the last checkpoint, each reading in full and what each copy's answer can touch. Do not restate a reading's meanings in the checkpoint:\n\n   ```text\n   hyp checkpoint --goal <goal-id> --summary \"Stopped: <stop clause>. Waiting for the owner: <the keys under AWAITING OWNER>. Out-of-Goal findings: <one short phrase each, or none>\" --why \"<why>\"\n   ```\n\n   An out-of-Goal finding is a case of none of the Goal's lines: it gets no limit, no refer and no reading, and it never holds the select. Name each one in the checkpoint so it lives in the log, not only in chat. A defect in text you changed and may edit is not one: fix it with a commit before the stop.\n\n2. Your last message repeats the checkpoint, and lists each line under LINES by its key, with the Results that ran it, and names each question where OWED still owes a line. A run that ends with lines owed is a normal result.\n\n3. Then list every owner item with the exact command the owner runs: each item under AWAITING OWNER, each out-of-Goal finding (a new Goal or an issue), merging the PR, reinstalling hyp, and each personal skill that names a command the change altered.\n\n## Never\n\n- Never change the Goal or its met rule.\n- Never record a choice the owner reserved, and never record an owner answer unless the owner gave those words in this session.\n(eval):1: no matches found: internal/prompt*","is_error":false},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0179Nqx7zFmKzbjNx9uD5rq6","type":"tool_result","content":"# Map: core and self-review\n\nThis repository holds hyp, and a few recipes and trees that test it. Records made before the public release stay in the private development history. This page says which concepts and files belong to which scope, so that an issue, a plan or a change can say which scope it serves. The two words are defined in [glossary.md](glossary.md), \"Scopes of this repository\".\n\n## The test\n\nCore and self-review are scopes. An item's scope is the concept it belongs to, not who uses it or what tested it.\n\n- **Core** is every concept hyp has, and everything a user who installs hyp into another repository runs, loads or reads there. A user writes recipes, runs sandboxes and records trees for their own code, so recipe, sandbox, store cut, runner and instrument are core concepts, even where only self-review uses them today.\n- **Self-review** is the recipes this repository keeps to test hyp, whatever repository the runner works on, plus the inputs only they use. Nothing else is self-review.\n\nThe repository a runner works on is its investigated repo, a core concept (spec §1). Here it was often hyp itself, and often a real outside project: session-replay's snapshots were sessions of a runner working on another application. Which repository it is does not change the scope.\n\nInvestigating hyp is an activity, not a scope. A tree takes its place by its question:\n\n- A tree whose question is about hyp takes that question's scope. Most trees here ask whether a core concept works, so they are core-scope work, carried out with self-review recipes.\n- A tree whose question is about another project is a self-review input: a run this repository keeps as evidence about hyp, not for its answer.\n\nThree rules keep the boundary honest:\n\n1. **A concept stays core when self-review is what tests it.** The runner was measured by session-replay, and it is still a core concept.\n2. **A self-review recipe does not make its kind self-review.** session-replay was a recipe; recipes are core.\n3. **Self-review invents no concept of its own.** Every word a self-review recipe uses maps onto core concepts (see \"Self-review words\" below). Work whose question is about a self-review recipe itself is a special case, and it needs a reason. Today there is one: an instrument must give its known answers before its verdicts count, which is a core rule applied to a self-review recipe.\n\nThere are exactly two scopes. An item that fits neither is a problem that has not been scoped well enough: split it until each part has one scope. `.agentkit/` is not an item of this project at all: it is the local development setup for working on the repository, like `.gitignore`.\n\n## Visibility\n\nVisibility is a separate axis from scope. Every item in this repository is public. A self-review row below marks its own visibility.\n\n## Self-review\n\nThe self-review recipes kept before the public release (session-replay, scratch-pg, readout-read, containment-arms, declaration-shape and signals-replay-proto), their inputs, and the trees that pinned them are not published. Records made before the public release stay in the private development history.\n\nWhen a question needs an instrument, it is a recipe with known answers that measures, and a rubric in `rubrics/` that turns its measures into verdicts, both pinned by the Experiments that use them (ADR 0018). A new self-review recipe gets a row here, with its visibility.\n\n| Item | Why it is self-review | Visibility |\n|---|---|---|\n| `recipes/id-citation-arms@1` | a recipe this repository keeps to test hyp: it runs the id-citation rule prototypes on a frozen fixture store for `investigations/id-citation`; it is no replay instrument | public |\n| `recipes/id-citation-arms@2` | a recipe this repository keeps to test hyp: version 1's cases and verdict, with every Arm built in Launch from the sandbox base or the base plus one patch from `investigations/id-citation/arms/`, and the fixture carried in the bundle; it is no replay instrument | public |\n| `investigations/id-citation/arms/` | an input only self-review recipes use: the id-citation Arms as patches over a main commit (`today`, `text-warn`, `cite`; `text-refuse` is the base), which `id-citation-arms@2` takes as `--patch` | public |\n| `recipes/authoring-stops@2` | a recipe this repository keeps to test hyp: it probes whether each stop an author meets while turning a method into an instrument is still live; the tree that pinned it is not published | public |\n| `recipes/recipe-authoring-arms@4` | a recipe this repository keeps to test hyp: a sonnet runner turns the id-citation method into an instrument on two hyp arms; it runs live runners, it does not replay a session; the tree that pinned it is not published | public |\n| `recipes/recipe-base-arms@1` | a recipe this repository keeps to test hyp: a sonnet runner builds a held-out instrument from the skill-text gate method in its bundle on three hyp arms (template only, a coarse base, fine bases), for `investigations/recipe-base`; copied by hand from a version of `recipe-authoring-arms` (its `recipe.md` names it by hash), because `--from` copies the same name only: the one bootstrap exception to starting from a base, had a base shipped (ADR 0009) | public |\n\n## Core\n\nEvery item is core: `cmd/`, `internal/`, `go.mod`, `go.sum`, the skills, and the docs in this directory, the specs [replay-spec.md](replay-spec.md) and [sandbox-spec.md](sandbox-spec.md) among them. The test data under `internal/*/testdata` is core too: synthetic stores and bundles written by hyp for its own tests, such as the store in `internal/testutil/testdata/synth` and its `generate.sh` (that store is history from before ADR 0015, which a current hyp still replays).\n\nA new tree under `investigations/` that asks about hyp takes its question's scope, as the test above says. A tree that asks about a self-review recipe itself is the special case under rule 3, and its reason is instrument trust.\n\n`investigations/recipe-base` asks whether hyp should embed recipe bases, so it is core-scope work. Its Result R-H5H95P selected templates only (ADR 0009), so the base candidates it tested were not shipped. `investigations/recipe-base/arms.txt` names the prototype branch they were built on, which stays in the private development history.\n\n`investigations/checkpoint-store-design` and `investigations/checkpoint-store` share one question, hyp's shared store, so both are core-scope work. The first is a discovery Goal, G-73R704, that records the design (ADR 0020); the second is the build Goal that implements it, G-D1CH1R, whose candidate is a hyp commit. Neither pins a recipe.\n\n## Self-review words\n\nA word a self-review recipe uses is a name for core concepts. A new self-review word gets an entry here, and a word that maps onto no core concept is a sign that a core concept is missing.\n\n| Self-review word | In core terms |\n|---|---|\n| arm (id-citation-arms) | an Arm: a hyp binary built in Launch from main or main plus one rival's patch, named by sha256, the subject the cases run (version 1 read binaries built by hand from absolute paths) |\n| arm (recipe-authoring-arms) | an Arm: a hyp binary, main or main plus one rival's prototype patch, pinned as the sandbox's `--hyp` |\n| arm (recipe-base-arms) | an Arm: a hyp binary built from main, or from main plus the embed prototype and one rival's base candidates, pinned as the sandbox's `--hyp`; `null` embeds no base; the candidates were not shipped (ADR 0009) |\n| stop (authoring-stops, recipe-authoring-arms) | a point where an author turning a method into an instrument is held up; counted as a failing hyp command or a fingerprint in the bundle or transcript |\n\n## Where the scopes touch\n\n**Core tests read only core test data.** Core tests read the synthetic store in `internal/testutil/testdata/synth`: `TestRunTreesReplayWithoutRejections` and `TestRepositoryRecipePinsMatch` (check), `TestCutMatchesExpected` (store), `TestCreateStoreCut` (sandbox), the signals goldens, `TestAmendShowsTheNewDesign` and `TestSignalsReplaysOnce` (cli), and `TestReadFixture` (cmdrecord). `TestStackedScratchPg` stacks `internal/sandbox/testdata/recipes/scratch-pg@1`. A copy of the repository that holds only core passes `go test`.\n\n**Self-review text inside core docs.** The core part of each passage is the general rule; the self-review part is session-replay's versions and history.\n\n- `skills/hyp-verification/RECIPE-FORMAT.md`: the examples that name scratch-pg teach core rules with a recipe this repository no longer keeps, which is a choice of example rather than a split. The stacking example names a generic `harness@1`.\n- [sandbox-spec.md](sandbox-spec.md) §5.2 records choices made in design trees that are core-scope, so that is ordinary provenance.\n- [how-hyp-improves-itself.md](how-hyp-improves-itself.md), \"Guard the loop while it runs, not before\", describes the tripwire that stops a run; the session-replay recipe whose `tripwire.py` built it stays in the private development history.\n\nSkill text reaches replayed runs, so moving a passage out of the skills is its own change, a build Goal like any change to `skills/`. This page is the owner decision such a change can cite.\n3:The words hyp uses for its tree, its recipes and its sandboxes. The specs ([replay-spec.md](replay-spec.md), [sandbox-spec.md](sandbox-spec.md)) and the recipe format define the rules; this file only fixes which word means what, links the rule's section instead of restating it, and names the Go type that owns each concept where one exists. It also names the two scopes of this repository, core and self-review, and the test that puts an item in one; [core-and-self-review.md](core-and-self-review.md) maps the repository onto them.\n22:The name a write records as its author, from `--actor` or `HYP_ACTOR` ([spec §1](replay-spec.md#1-names-and-locations)). Each actor appends to its own events file, so logs from several worktrees merge ([spec §6](replay-spec.md#6-merge-order)). An actor is not a role: owner is the role an owner checkpoint names (the verifier role is retired, ADR 0015).\n46:A Goal whose candidate is a commit: building is a Goal like any other. A line asserts one property of the candidate, decided by its own tests or one pinned instrument: `[P]` pins what a valid input produces, and `[N]` pins that a forbidden state is refused. Each commit is a Hypothesis whose subject is the base plus that commit's change, and a fix is a new Hypothesis that supersedes it. Each Experiment's verdict rule asks for red on base and green on the candidate; the base of the red run's subject is where the build starts, and the Goal records no start commit. A finding of a run's review that is a case of a line is a limit; one that is not is an out-of-Goal finding. A change to a skill is a build Goal too (ADR 0021).\n144:Retired by ADR 0019. It was the `confirm` event in which a role judged a select `sufficient` or `insufficient`; the last use was the owner's confirm of a scope-change select. `hyp confirm` exits 2. An old owner confirm replays only the limits it accepted ([spec §4](replay-spec.md#4-event-types-closed-set)), and an old verifier confirm applies nothing. The owner still \"confirms\" in plain speech when they choose or accept.\n145:_Avoid_: approve, review; verdict (alone: a confirm's answer was a confirm verdict)\n154:**Verifier**:\n155:Retired by ADR 0015. It was the role that confirmed every select, independently of the actor who made it. Old verifier confirms replay as history and apply nothing, and `hyp confirm` is retired (ADR 0019). Any actor may still review: a defect is recorded as a Result, a retract or a limit. In code: `event.RoleVerifier`, read only from old logs.\n156:_Avoid_: reviewer, checker (as a role)\n179:A finding, often from a run's review, that is a case of none of the Goal's lines and asks no question about what a line means. It is not a limit, since a limit is a case the Goal covers, so it gets no tree event, no refer and no accept, and it never holds the select. The runner names it in its stop checkpoint's summary and its last message as a possible new Goal or issue, and the owner decides at global scope whether to add one. A defect in text the run changed and may edit is not one: the run fixes it with a commit.\n258:**verifier-loop**:\n259:Retired with the verifier role (ADR 0015). It was a select that collected two or more insufficient verifier verdicts in one chain.\n438:Every concept hyp has, and everything a user who installs hyp into another repository runs, loads or reads there. A concept stays core when self-review is what tests it.\n441:**Self-review**:\n442:The recipes this repository keeps to test hyp, whatever repository the runner works on, with the inputs only they use. Investigating hyp is an activity, not a scope: a tree about hyp takes the scope of its question, and a tree about another project is a self-review input.","is_error":false}]}}
