on: a limit is a case the Goal covers that no check ran. If a test within the Goal's lines can run the case on every candidate, add an Experiment with `--aims` at it, run it, and record `--closes` on the Result that ran the case. A close records that the case ran, whatever the verdicts: a candidate that fails the case is contradicted and the limit is still closed, so never write a rule that closes it only on a pass. A close covers every case the limit's text names; `hyp record` prints that text when it closes. Record each case you did not run as a new `--limit` on the closing Result, and refer that new key when its case is void or outside the Goal's lines. A claim that a case is void, out of scope or covered elsewhere is a limit and a refer, never a sentence in a body or an observation. When that claim rests on your reading of a Goal line, put the reading in the refer's text. Write one case per `--limit`. If no such test can, or the case is void for every candidate, refer it with your reason: `hyp checkpoint --refers \"<key>=<why no test within the Goal can close it, or why the case is void>\" --why \"...\"`; hyp refuses a refer with no reason. When the owner returns a referred limit, it is an ordinary `limit` line again.\n5. End with the candidates left under each Question: none, one, or several, by alias.\n6. Select only when OWED lists `select` for that Question: `hyp select <Q-id> <H-id> --why \"...\"`. Never confirm a select. Do not change the Goal or add new structures unless the owner asks.\n\n## Goal lines\n\n- The Goal's lines are limits keyed on the Goal, `G-<id>#<n>`; `hyp status` prints them under LINES. A Goal's lines come with its add and no later event adds one. If the Goal's body states lines and none is typed, they cannot be added to this Goal: tell the owner, who may add a new Goal with them (`hyp add goal … --line \"<line>\"`). Do not write them for the owner.\n- A line is owed as a `limit` on each top-level Question with a candidate no closer covers. Run its case like any limit: aim at it with `--aims G-<id>#<n>`, and record `--closes G-<id>#<n>` on each Result that ran the case. A Result covers a candidate it gave a verdict on, or one whose whole subject (base, patch and files) one of the Result's cited runs holds, so tie each candidate before the run: `hyp subject <H> [PATH…] --base <commit> [--patch FILE]`.\n- A line is never edited. What a line means is a reading. A line no test within the Goal can run is a refer, and the owner accepts or returns it. A new line or a new purpose is a new Goal, so a new tree: tell the owner, and do not write it.\n\n## Stop\n\nStop on the clauses the `/goal` prompt names. Stopping is not success.\n\n1. Before your last message, record a runner checkpoint, so the fork does not live only in chat; the owner reads `hyp status`, which prints the last checkpoint, each reading in full and what each copy's answer can touch. Do not restate a reading's meanings in the checkpoint:\n\n   ```text\n   hyp checkpoint --summary \"Stopped: <stop clause>. Waiting for the owner: <the keys under AWAITING OWNER>. Out-of-Goal findings: <one short phrase each, or none>\" --why \"<why>\"\n   ```\n\n   An out-of-Goal finding is a case of none of the Goal's lines: it gets no limit, no refer and no reading, and it never holds the select. Name each one in the checkpoint so it lives in the log, not only in chat. A defect in text you changed and may edit is not one: fix it with a commit before the stop.\n\n2. Your last message repeats the checkpoint, and lists each line under LINES by its key, with the Results that ran it, and names each question where OWED still owes a line. A run that ends with lines owed is a normal result.\n\n## Never\n\n- Never change the Goal or its met rule.\n- Never record a choice the owner reserved, and never record an owner answer unless the owner gave those words in this session.\n---\nstatus: accepted\n---\n\nSupersedes in part [ADR 0007](0007-records-start-at-230.md) (the skill-text gate's replay instrument), [ADR 0010](0010-a-record-keeps-or-identifies-what-a-replay-needs.md) (the follow-up that rebuilds `skill-text-arms` Arms from patches) and [ADR 0015](0015-the-select-is-the-gate.md) (the frozen verifier recipes).\n\n# A skill change is a build Goal\n\nAGENTS.md gated skill text apart from code. A skill-text change shipped only when a session replay from the event's store cut showed the failure in 3 or more of 5 trials without the change and in 1 or fewer of 5 with it, and nothing shipped when no cut reproduced the failure. Its one instrument, `recipes/skill-text-arms@5` with the rubric `rubrics/skill-text-gate@1`, replays the id-citation tree's e-arms with `hyp-prototype` and `hyp-verification` loaded. It cannot reach a skill that runs before any tree exists, such as `hyp-owner-grill`, or one that spans a whole `/goal` run, such as `hyp-goal-run` and `hyp-owner-answer`. For those skills the rule was a permanent freeze, and a new skill needed its own exception. [how-hyp-improves-itself.md](../how-hyp-improves-itself.md) had already found that replay studies of skill text decided nothing, because agents with the skill loaded rarely made the mistakes they counted.\n\nOwner decision on 2026-11-11 (#134): building is a Goal like any other (ADR 0020), and a change to `skills/` is building. The special gate was context that led agents to design gate policy instead of applying that model.\n\n## Decision\n\n- **A change to `skills/`, a new skill included, is a build Goal like any code change.** Its candidate is the commit; its lines state the skill's behaviour; each line is checked by its own test or by an instrument the Goal builds, red on base, and the select ends it. For a new skill, base is no skill.\n- **How a skill is tested is that Goal's choice**, settled in its grill. One choice is ak-skill-creator's eval: cases in its `evals.json` format kept as a file of a `recipes/<skill>-eval@N` bundle, graded by `eval_skill.py`, which is named on the command's `External:` line and never copied. hyp keeps no instrument and no threshold for skill text as such.\n- **A skill error seen during another run** is an out-of-Goal finding, or a new Goal by promotion. No audit cadence is kept.\n- **Unchanged:** a change still needs a real event, and a failure detectable from the tree goes into the binary as a refusal or a warning, gated by `go test`.\n- **Removed:** `recipes/skill-text-arms@3`, `@4` and `@5`, `rubrics/skill-text-gate@1`, and the retired `recipes/verifier-brief-arms@1` and `recipes/verifier-audit-arms@1`.\n\n## Alternatives\n\n- **Replay from eval cases instead of a store cut, keeping 3 of 5 against 1 of 5.** Rejected: it keeps skill text apart from code, and the count is a test design that belongs to the Goal.\n- **A standing audit after each real use of a skill.** Rejected: an error seen in a run already has a home, as an out-of-Goal finding or a new Goal.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- No published tree pins a removed recipe. `investigations/recipe-base` names `skill-text-arms` only as the held-out subject its runners authored, and `recipes/recipe-base-arms@1` carries its own copy of that method.\n- The removed bundles stay in git history; an old Result that names one can still be read from there.\n- `id-citation-arms`, `recipe-base-arms`, `recipe-authoring-arms` and `authoring-stops` are kept.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sandboxViolations":[],"sandboxed":true,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"748bc978-ee9d-4d4f-b2e0-cbf670d30b8d","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"rule","ruleSource":"cliArg"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-cli","cwd":"/Users/datguy/.hyp-sandbox/g146-base-a-1/wt","sessionId":"ea4155e0-5290-43bb-8480-42acd6eefa1d","version":"2.1.322","gitBranch":"work"}
