+{"parentUuid":"fa5fab5e-4bb2-4577-b257-d432202aebb5","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ad104d8b-74b3-452c-b5aa-0618caa2eec4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MppEKaqvBFh6esL9HuqSEB","type":"tool_result","content":"28:`hyp bundle run` and `hyp bundle known-answers` find REF in `recipes/` or `rubrics/`; a rubric is a bundle with one Standalone command and no step (replay spec §7.6), so `hyp bundle evidence` refuses it with exit 2, and a ref found in both libraries is refused with exit 1. `hyp recipe` runs as a hidden alias of `hyp bundle`, because frozen bundles call it from their scripts (ADR 0018).\n32:A sandbox is a directory plus its record, not process isolation. Each command hyp starts leads its own process group, which its row records as `pgid` (ADR 0002). Before Cleanup, `hyp sandbox remove` stops the groups of commands a killed hyp left unfinished (§4); after Cleanup, it stops the groups of the Launch, Doctor, Drive and Cleanup commands that still have members: SIGTERM, up to 5 seconds, then SIGKILL. A timeout or Ctrl-C kills the running command's group (its row says `interrupted` for Ctrl-C), and an Evidence or Standalone group ends with its command; create still runs Cleanup after a Ctrl-C. Limits: a daemon that leaves its group, as Postgres's postmaster does by calling `setsid`, still needs its recipe's Cleanup, and the leftover check still applies; a group id reused after its group ended is not told apart; what a check cannot see is listed under [replay-spec.md](replay-spec.md) §7.6 and ADR 0003. \"Runs in a sandbox\" means only that the command runs while the sandbox exists and gets that sandbox's `HYP_SANDBOX_*` variables (§1.3).\n36:hyp runs every command in a bundle, and the operator runs none of a bundle's scripts directly. A command is what a `Run:` line names; every other file in the bundle, a helper script included, is hashed content (ADR 0002). Hashing never parses: a bundle whose `recipe.md` breaks a command rule, such as a `Run:` line outside a section or shell syntax, still hashes, so every pin to it still verifies, and `hyp check` names it as a bundle that does not open. `hyp bundle new` writes `format: 1` into every bundle it makes, a first version from its embedded template (`hyp bundle new NAME@1`) as well as a copy (`--from`), the one recipe format, and the line is a required field. Every use of a bundle without it is refused with exit 1, reading only its frontmatter: `hyp add exp`, `hyp sandbox create`, `hyp bundle run`, `hyp bundle evidence`, `hyp bundle known-answers`, and `hyp record --evidence` into such a bundle. The refusal names `hyp bundle new` as the way to copy it. `hyp bundle hash` still hashes it (ADR 0002, ADR 0007). `hyp bundle check` names each versioned bundle `frozen` when a tree pins its current hash, so its bytes never change, and `draft` when none does. When the repository root holds `go.mod`, `hyp bundle check` warns about each `.go` file in a versioned bundle, and `hyp add exp --recipe` about each one in the bundle it pins, because `go vet ./...` reads a `recipes/<name>@<N>/` directory holding one as a package outside the main module; the warning suggests a non-`.go` name, such as `x.go.txt`, and both commands still exit 0.\n40:Each Evidence and Standalone command states its reach on one line `External: TEXT` directly under its `Run:` line in `recipe.md`: the subject arguments and any outside reads or writes, or `none`. The line is no field `recipe.Load` requires, so a frozen bundle without it still loads and records. `hyp bundle check` warns `<command>: no External: line` for a command without one and `<command>: External: not stated (TODO)` for one whose text is empty or `TODO`, and exits as before. `hyp bundle new` writes `External: TODO` under each such command of a copy that has no line, and under the Standalone command of a first version. Every line of a first version's template that its author must replace holds the marker `hyp:placeholder`, and `hyp add exp --recipe` refuses (exit 1) a bundle that still holds it in any file outside a known answer's `input/`, naming the files (ADR 0009).\n44:### 1.3 How a command runs\n119:**Limits.** A group id reused after its group ended is not told apart from it. So an unrelated group holding a recorded unfinished id refuses a drive, whose refusal says remove still works, and remove can signal it (§1.1). hyp does not check for a killed run's scratch outside the sandbox (§1.3). `hyp bundle evidence` does not read how far setup got; the kept record's `setup` and rows show it, and #46 holds the open question.\n128:- **Command record.** For each run of Evidence or Standalone commands, hyp writes a command record in the new directory that `--record DIR` names (refused with exit 2 if it exists, is or resolves inside a `recipes/` directory the recipe lookup searches, or, for Evidence, inside OUT_DIR, comparing directories by identity so symlinks and other spellings do not get past it; with exit 3 if its path cannot be resolved): `command.json`, naming the recipe ref, its hash, the library path and the working directory (each `<repo>/REL`, see Kept inputs), the step, with one row per command (its arguments as shown, its exit code or why it has none, and when it ran) `inputs`, each file an operator argument names, and `dirs`, each directory one names, with its tree's hash and each file's (format 6, so `hyp record` can copy them into the run's subject on a Result); `command.log`, the output after `== REF step: command` lines; and each check's product, its stdout in `stdout` and its `$HYP_OUT` tree in `out/` (Standalone), or `stdout/<n>` and `out/<n>/` (Evidence command `n`), kept even when the check fails (ADR 0003). The fields are listed under \"The command record\" in `RECIPE-FORMAT.md`.\n135:- **One hyp command per sandbox.** `hyp sandbox create`, `drive` and `remove` hold a lock on `sandbox.lock` in the sandbox while they run. A second one on the same sandbox is refused with exit 3, naming the hyp command that holds it, and changes nothing. The lock goes away when its hyp exits, however it exits. `hyp bundle evidence` and `hyp bundle run` take no lock, because each writes only the new directory it made.\ninternal/replay/validate.go:85:\t\tmaxBody = event.MaxGoalBody // a Goal's body holds the owner's met rule\n296:\tvar warns []string\n388:\t\t\tif warns, err = admitted(w.st.AdmitExp(p, facts, rubricFacts, noTarget)); err != nil {\n406:\t\tfor _, l := range warns {\n414:// or bad-args finding (exit 2) is the error, and each warning becomes a\n415:// \"warn: \" line to print once the event is written.\n416:func admitted(fs []replay.Finding) (warns []string, err error) {\n424:\t\t\twarns = append(warns, \"warn: \"+f.Text)\n427:\treturn warns, nil\n141:func Commands() []string {\n142-\tnames := []string{\"version\", \"help\"}\n143-\tfor name := range commands {\n144-\t\tnames = append(names, name)\n145-\t}\n146-\tsort.Strings(names)\n147-\treturn names\n148-}\n149-\n150-// Run executes one hyp invocation and returns the process exit code: 0\n151-// success, 1 refused, 2 usage error, 3 I/O or internal error. It is the only\n152-// place a herr kind becomes an exit code.\n153-func Run(args []string, stdout, stderr io.Writer) int {\n# Map: core and self-review\n\nThis repository holds hyp, and a few recipes and trees that test it. Records made before the public release stay in the private development history. This page says which concepts and files belong to w\n\n## The test\n\nCore and self-review are scopes. An item's scope is the concept it belongs to, not who uses it or what tested it.\n\n- **Core** is every concept hyp has, and everything a user who installs hyp into another repository runs, loads or reads there. A user writes recipes, runs sandboxes and records trees for their own co\n- **Self-review** is the recipes this repository keeps to test hyp, whatever repository the runner works on, plus the inputs only they use. Nothing else is self-review.\n\nThe repository a runner works on is its investigated repo, a core concept (spec §1). Here it was often hyp itself, and often a real outside project: session-replay's snapshots were sessions of a runne\n\nInvestigating hyp is an activity, not a scope. A tree takes its place by its question:\n\n- A tree whose question is about hyp takes that question's scope. Most trees here ask whether a core concept works, so they are core-scope work, carried out with self-review recipes.\n- A tree whose question is about another project is a self-review input: a run this repository keeps as evidence about hyp, not for its answer.\n\nThree rules keep the boundary honest:\n\n1. **A concept stays core when self-review is what tests it.** The runner was measured by session-replay, and it is still a core concept.\n2. **A self-review recipe does not make its kind self-review.** session-replay was a recipe; recipes are core.\n3. **Self-review invents no concept of its own.** Every word a self-review recipe uses maps onto core concepts (see \"Self-review words\" below). Work whose question is about a self-review recipe itself\n\nThere are exactly two scopes. An item that fits neither is a problem that has not been scoped well enough: split it until each part has one scope. `.agentkit/` is not an item of this project at all: i\n\n## Visibility\n\nVisibility is a separate axis from scope. Every item in this repository is public. A self-review row below marks its own visibility.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"0108126c-3a54-461e-99b8-5cf75660029c","timestamp":"2026-11-12T14:20:01.728Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"28:`hyp bundle run` and `hyp bundle known-answers` find REF in `recipes/` or `rubrics/`; a rubric is a bundle with one Standalone command and no step (replay spec §7.6), so `hyp bundle evidence` refuses it with exit 2, and a ref found in both libraries is refused with exit 1. `hyp recipe` runs as a hidden alias of `hyp bundle`, because frozen bundles cal