+{"parentUuid":"6616e57e-cfe2-4e28-b72f-e28d4b80560f","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"167e9bf2-bfac-4933-9d34-c1082a9ac85d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MVaAYpkLtETNiLxKUhzCco","type":"tool_result","content":"- **The ledger.** `hyp status` lists under OWED what the tree owes and who\n  owes it, and marks an item an Experiment aimed at and did not settle\n  ([replay-spec.md](replay-spec.md) §8.1). It is the one account of progress\n  ([ADR 0017](adr/0017-the-ledger-is-the-stall-guard.md)). A run's cap is\n  stated by the `/goal` prompt or the recipe, not by hyp.\n- **Design marks.** `hyp signals` lists the Experiments set up against no live\n  rival (one-rival) or against a dead one (D1). A Result that leaves a\n\n- **`wasted`, `pending`, `no-movement` and `spin` are removed.** A second account of progress either agrees with the ledger, and adds nothing, or disagrees with it, and is a false alarm.\n- **`one-rival`, `D1` and `unjudged` stay.** Each is already a write-time refusal or warning, or a mark that `hyp status` and `hyp show` print. `hyp signals` lists them for the whole tree.\n- **`hyp signals --json` keeps the facts of each round:** its Experiment, cut, parent, targets, question, statuses at add, its marks and its Results with their status moves. It drops `pending`, `wasted`, `closed`, `line` and `moved`.\n- **A run's cap stays outside hyp.** A `/goal` prompt or a recipe states how many Experiments a run may take.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Old trees print fewer signal lines. No event, payload, status or ledger item changes.\nFrom there the [hyp-prototype skill](skills/hyp-prototype/SKILL.md) guides the next rounds: experiments, verdicts, selects and checks. When `hyp status` lists something under AWAITING OWNER, it is yours to answer. [example.sh](skills/hyp-prototype/example.sh) runs a complete investigation in a throwaway directory.\n\n### The owner flow: grill, run, answer\n\nFor a Goal you want run unattended, discovery or build, three skills split the work. They run in Claude Code, whose `/goal` command keeps a session going until its condition holds.\n\n1. **`/hyp-owner-grill`** questions you about the purpose until it is a Goal a run can finish. It looks up the facts itself, sends questions a run could test to DesignQuestions with rivals, derives the lines from what must and must never happen, splits purposes that need different candidates into separate Goals, and records your writes only after you approve them. It ends with a `/goal` prompt.\n2. **`/goal <that prompt>`** runs the Goal with the `hyp-goal-run` skill. The run does only what OWED says it owes, tests before it asks, and stops with a report you can read from `hyp status`.\n3. **`/hyp-owner-answer`** walks you through what the stopped run left under AWAITING OWNER, explains each item and records only your answers. The next `/goal` run picks up from there.\n\nTo work on hyp itself, see [install.md, \"Develop hyp\"](docs/install.md#develop-hyp).\n\n## What is in this repository\n23:- Actor: the `--actor` flag, or the `HYP_ACTOR` environment variable. It must match `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,39}$`. If it is missing, every write command fails \n31:- Resolving a user-typed reference, in order: an exact ULID, then an alias, then a display id. Zero matches gives `unknown node <ref>`. Two or more matches g\n67:**[added, then removed from use] `confirm` with `role`**: §3 required a verifier check on every select and an owner check on trade-offs, and replay can only \n71:**[added] limits**: a Result names the limits it found (`limits`): each is a case the Goal covers that no check ran on the Hypotheses the Result judged (ADR \n86:**[added] lines**: the Goal's lines are the cases every candidate must meet (ADR 0016 amendment \"a Goal line is a limit the owner records\"). They come with t\n88:**[added] subject**: a subject has one shape, a base commit plus what is given over it: at most one patch, and input files (ADR 0020). A Hypothesis may name \n90:**[added] readings**: a Result names a line of the Goal that a verdict rests on and how the run read it (`readings`). A reading's key is `<result-id>#r<n>`, \n124:23. A `checkpoint` or `answer` move on a reading or a limit that the move tables of §8.2 refuse. The refusals that name `closes` and `revises` below read ol\n136:CLI error text is `refused: <reason>` on stderr, with exit code 1. A help request (`-h` or `--help` on any command, and `help` at the top level and on a gro\n144:- `hyp confirm` is retired (ADR 0019): it exits 2 before anything else, whatever its flags, naming what replaces a scope change: the runner refers the Goal \n145:- `hyp add exp` under a Hypothesis is refused when the Experiment has no rubric: `--rubric REF` pins a bundle, `--rubric reading` says the body decides its \n146:- `hyp record` is refused, at exit 1, when an `--evidence` pointer names a file under `.tree/evidence/` that is neither in its Experiment's own run nor amon","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d72efccc-ffce-424e-80b9-78be1ce3ed34","timestamp":"2026-11-12T14:22:53.771Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"- **The ledger.** `hyp status` lists under OWED what the tree owes and who\n  owes it, and marks an item an Experiment aimed at and did not settle\n  ([replay-spec.md](replay-spec.md) §8.1). It is the one account of progress\n  ([ADR 0017](adr/0017-the-ledger-is-the-stall-guard.md)). A run's cap is\n  stated by the `/goal` prompt or the recipe, not by hyp.\n- **Design marks.** `hyp signals` lists the Experiments set up against no live\n  rival (one-rival) or against a dead one (D1). A Result that leaves a\n\n- **`wasted`, `pending`, `no-movement` and `spin` are removed.** A second account of progress either agrees with the ledger, and adds nothing, or disagrees with it, and is a false alarm.\n- **`one-rival`, `D1` and `unjudged` stay.** Each is already a write-time refusal or warning, or a mark that `hyp status` and `hyp show` print. `hyp signals` lists them f