+{"parentUuid":"a20764db-a2bb-4df8-bc1f-031a9b27f1cd","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f628c061-01ce-4e4c-bfd9-39705e2e89d2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018LZe5mQGWUTbKnvJxtWGdX","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (35KB). Full output saved to: /Users/datguy/.claude/projects/-Users-datguy--hyp-sandbox-g146-base-a-2-wt/e3ce0dbb-c168-4a49-9caf-ecc01cb40fbf/tool-results/bf5a05zef.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n---\nstatus: proposed\n---\n\n# One store holds many Goals\n\nUntil now a tree had one Goal (spec §5 rule 4: \"only one Goal per log\"). A new purpose was a new tree, and that tree cited the old one in prose as `<tree>:ID`, which hyp does not check (ADR 0016). After the multi-tenant tree completed on 2026-11-10, the owner wanted the build that follows a tree to run from the tree instead of from a plan in prose. Every problem with that came from the one-Goal rule:\n\n- A build had no checked way to cite what the tree had decided.\n- The owner's checkpoints were split across trees.\n- A commit could not be a subject, because `hyp record` copied file hashes and dropped the sandbox's `base` (`internal/cli/subject.go:118`).\n\nIn session `e6a35a24` (2026-11-10 and 11) the owner settled the model: **building is a Goal like any other.** hyp keeps no discovery kind and no delivery kind. The design questions it raised are recorded and selected in `investigations/checkpoint-store-design` (G-73R704), with that session as evidence:\n\n- Q-D906CD (R-HW9B05): what a Result keeps about its runs.\n- Q-HC4V9T (R-V8032Z): where a build Goal's start commit lives.\n- Q-H8PJ20 (R-8CJCV6): what the owner's act is attached to.\n- Q-R21S8R (R-AWPQ4X): how many event types there are.\n\n`investigations/checkpoint-store` (G-D1CH1R) builds the design, and its nine owner lines are this ADR's acceptance. Tracked in #126, part of #114.\n\n## Decision\n\n- **One store holds many Goals.** The store is still one `.tree/` with one events file per actor. A tree is a view, not a container: a Goal and every node whose parents lead to it. Rule 4's \"only one Goal per log\" goes.\n- **A subject has one shape: a base commit, plus what is given over it.** What is given over it is at most one patch, and input files. This holds for every sense of the word:\n  - an Experiment's subject is what its sandbox assembles;\n  - a run's subject is the base, the one patch and the input files its records name;\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"1c7fe12b-ffca-4ad2-a806-f8bb35e05d14","timestamp":"2026-11-12T14:19:50.765Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\nstatus: proposed\n---\n\n# One store holds many Goals\n\nUntil now a tree had one Goal (spec §5 rule 4: \"only one Goal per log\"). A new purpose was a new tree, and that tree cited the old one in prose as `<tree>:ID`, which hyp does not check (ADR 0016). After the multi-tenant tree completed on 2026-11-10, the owner wanted the build that follows a tree to run from the tree instead of from a plan in prose. Every problem with that came from the one-Goal rule:\n\n- A build had no checked way to cite what the tree had decided.\n- The owner's checkpoints were split across trees.\n- A commit could not be a subject, because `hyp record` copied file hashes and dropped the sandbox's `base` (`internal/cli/subject.go:118`).\n\nIn session `e6a35a24` (2026-11-10 and 11) the owner settled the model: **building is a Goal like any other.** hyp keeps no discovery kind and no delivery kind. The design questions it raised are recorded and selected in `investigations/checkpoint-store-design` (G-73R704), with that session as evidence:\n\n- Q-D906CD (R-HW9B05): what a Result keeps about its runs.\n- Q-HC4V9T (R-V8032Z): where a build Goal's start commit lives.\n- Q-H8PJ20 (R-8CJCV6): what the owner's act is attached to.\n- Q-R21S8R (R-AWPQ4X): how many event types there are.\n\n`investigations/checkpoint-store` (G-D1CH1R) builds the design, and its nine owner lines are this ADR's acceptance. Tracked in #126, part of #114.\n\n## Decision\n\n- **One store holds many Goals.** The store is still one `.tree/` with one events file per actor. A tree is a view, not a container: a Goal and every node whose parents lead to it. Rule 4's \"only one Goal per log\" goes.\n- **A subject has one shape: a base commit, plus what is given over it.** What is given over it is at most one patch, and input files. This holds for every sense of the word:\n  - an Experiment's subject is what its sandbox assembles;\n  - a run's subject is the base, the one patch and the input files its records name;\n  - a Hypothesis's subject may name a base and at most one patch over it, and names files by sha256.\n\n  A Result keeps **one subject per cited run**, `{base, patch, files}`, instead of one merged `fingerprints` list. A Hypothesis is covered when one entry holds its whole subject, so a run at B plus a patch never covers bare B. An old Result reads as one entry with no base. The word `fingerprints` stays for the hashed files inside a subject (Q-D906CD).\n- **A Goal records no start commit.** A build Goal's start is the base of its red-on-base Result's subject (Q-HC4V9T).\n- **A Goal's lines come with the Goal.** The owner adds the Goal with `lines: [{text, cites}]` in its `node.add`, at most 10 lines of 1 to 600 runes. No later event adds a line.\n- **A cite is a plain key, 0 to 10 per line.** Each must be settled when the line is written:\n  - a line;\n  - an accepted or closed limit;\n  - an answered reading;\n  - a selected or contradicted Hypothesis.\n\n  Anything else is refused with exit 1. A cite carries no relation label. Its meaning is derived from the key's state at the line's position in the log:\n\n  | The cited key, when the line was written | The cite means |\n  |---|---|\n  | a line, or an accepted limit | carried |\n  | a closed limit, or a contradicted Hypothesis | forbidden |\n  | an answered reading | applied |\n  | a selected Hypothesis | realised |\n\n  A Goal's origin is the Goals its cited keys belong to. When a cited key's state later differs, `hyp check` warns and `hyp show` prints both states; no status changes.\n- **The owner's acts are their own event type, `answer`.** It holds `answers`, `accepts` and `returns`, each `[{key, text}]`, with at least one key. It has no `node`, no `summary`, no envelope `why` and no `role`. The owner's reason is in each item's text (Q-R21S8R, Q-H8PJ20).\n  - An answer settles an item Awaiting owner lists, or replaces the owner's own earlier answer.\n  - Whether an item in `answers` is a first answer or a revision is derived from the reading's state, so `closes` and `revises` merge.\n  - `hyp answer` warns, at exit 0, when it revises a key another Goal's line cites.\n  - An accept means only \"this Goal does not run this case\". A later Goal carries the case by citing the accepted key, and there is no deferred state.\n- **The runner's `checkpoint` keeps its Goal as `node` and a required `why`.** It holds `summary` and `refers: [{key, text}]`, and at least one of the two. An empty `checkpoint` is refused.\n- **Whose hand an item is in is the contract between the two event types, and OWED is a view of it.** The move tables (`internal/replay/keyed.go`) enforce the hand on the stored log and in a merged log. No write is checked against OWED.\n- **A move never crosses Goals; a cite or a reading's `of` may.**\n  - A refer must lead to its checkpoint's Goal.\n  - `aims`, `closes` and `applies` must lead to their Experiment's Goal.\n\n  A later Goal takes a case over by citing it, never by moving it.\n- **Status across Goals:**\n  - OWED is per Goal.\n  - Awaiting owner is store-level, grouped by Goal.\n  - `hyp status G-…` shows one Goal.\n  - `hyp status` prints today's output in a one-Goal store, and in a store with many Goals a GOALS table and one Awaiting owner list.\n  - `LastCheckpoint` is per Goal and counts runner checkpoints only.\n- **A free-text owner approval has no event.** A runner that needs the owner's word records a reading, and the owner answers it.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- **Breaking, amended by the owner decision of 2026-11-11.** The break is in what commands write, not in what replay reads. An old checkpoint's `lines`, `role`, `closes`, `revises`, `accepts` and `returns` stay read-only history that replay still applies and no command writes, as `confirm` is in spec §4; its `refers`, stored as bare keys, decode as refers with no text. G-D1CH1R's own lines are stored that way, on its first owner checkpoint, and keep their keys. `hyp checkpoint` no longer takes `--role`, `--closes`, `--revises`, `--accept`, `--returns` or `--line` (exit 2, naming `hyp answer` or `hyp add goal --line`). In the public trees, id-citation and recipe-base, each checkpoint holds only a `summary`, so they replay unchanged. G-D1CH1R line 1 is that census.\n- **Order of the build.** Two-Goal stores land first. Before that, a test of a cross-Goal rule fails while building its two-Goal fixture, which is red for the wrong reason. Each negative-space line gets a test pair: a refused write, and a rejected replay of a log holding the forbidden event. Each half fails on the commit before its own change, with a message naming its rule (G-D1CH1R line 9).\n- **Superseded text, changed in the PRs that build it:**\n  - principles §5 (\"a new purpose is a new Goal, so a new tree\") and §7 rule 1 (\"its lines are written on one owner checkpoint … so a new tree\");\n  - the glossary's Goal, Line, Subject, Coverage and Checkpoint, plus a new Answer entry;\n  - ADR 0016's \"A new Goal is a new tree\" and its cross-tree cite rules;\n  - ADR 0019's table row \"a new Goal, so a new tree\";\n  - spec §4 (the `checkpoint` row, a new `answer` row, `lines` on `node.add`), §5 rules 4, 21 to 24, and §8, §8.1 and §8.2.\n\n  The skills follow under the recorded-decision exception, and each commit names this ADR.\n29:- Display id: `<K>-<last 6 characters of the ULID>`, where K is `G` (Goal), `Q` (DesignQuestion), `H` (Hypothesis), `E` (Experiment) or `R` (Result). Example: `H-7QK2ZD`.\n49:| `node.add` | the new id (= event id) | `kind` (Goal, DesignQuestion, Hypothesis, Experiment), `parent` (id; `\"\"` only for Goal), `title`, `body` (optional), `alias` (optional), `recipe` (Experiment only, required: `\"name@version\"` or `\"none\"`), `recipe_hash` (hex sha256, required when recipe ≠ none), `targets` (Experiment only, optional list of Hypothesis ids; none on an Experiment under a DesignQuestion), `decided_by` (Experiment only, optional: `\"reading\"`, the verdicts are decided by reading; history: replay reads it as `rubric: \"reading\"`, and no command writes it), `rubric` (Experiment only, optional: `\"name@version\"` of a rubric bundle, or `\"reading\"`, the verdicts are decided by reading the body), `rubric_hash` (hex sha256, required when `rubric` names a bundle), `records` (Experiment only, optional list of at most 500 objects `{\"path\": <1–300 runes>, \"sha256\": <lowercase hex sha256>}`, no path twice: the earlier records the Experiment reads beyond its own run), `aims` (Experiment only, optional list of at most 10 limit or reading keys the Experiment is planned to settle), `lines` (Goal only, optional list of at most 10 objects `{\"text\": <1–600 runes>, \"cites\": <optional list of at most 10 keys>}`: the Goal's lines, §4 lines) |\n62:| `checkpoint` | Goal id | the runner's report on its Goal: `summary` (optional, at most 600 runes), `refers` (optional list of at most 10 limit keys referred to the owner, each an object `{\"key\": <limit key>, \"text\": <1–600 runes, why no test within the Goal can settle it>}`, or a bare key, when `summary` says why); at least one of the two, and `why` is required. Read-only history (owner decision of 2026-11-11, ADR 0020): `role` (`owner`), `closes`, `revises`, `accepts`, `returns` and `lines`, as an old owner checkpoint wrote them; replay still applies them, as `confirm` is applied, and no command writes them: `closes` (open reading keys the owner answered with `summary`), `revises` (answered reading keys whose answer `summary` replaced), `accepts` (at most 10 referred limit keys accepted), `returns` (at most 10 referred or accepted limit keys returned), `lines` (at most 10 lines of 1–300 runes, only on the first owner checkpoint on its Goal that carries any). A limit key is a Result's `<result-id>#<n>` or a line's `<goal-id>#<n>` |\n63:| `answer` | none (`\"\"`) | the owner's act (ADR 0020): `answers`, `accepts` and `returns`, each an optional list of at most 10 objects `{\"key\": <key>, \"text\": <1–600 runes, the owner's words>}`, with at least one key in all. `answers` names reading keys: a first answer of an open reading, or a new answer of an answered one, derived from the reading's state. `accepts` names referred limit keys, and `returns` referred or accepted ones. One answer may name keys of several Goals. It has no `summary`, no `refers`, no `role`, no `node` and no `why` |\n67:**[added, then removed from use] `confirm` with `role`**: §3 required a verifier check on every select and an owner check on trade-offs, and replay can only be pure if both are stored events. The verifier role is retired (ADR 0015), and the owner confirm with it (ADR 0019): the select is the gate, and a scope change is a Goal line the owner accepts, or a new tree. `hyp confirm` exits 2. Old logs keep their confirms, and only an owner confirm's `accepts` applies.\n71:**[added] limits**: a Result names the limits it found (`limits`): each is a case the Goal covers that no check ran on the Hypotheses the Result judged (ADR 0016). A later Result that runs the case closes it (`closes`). A limit's key is `<result-id>#<n>`: the Result's full ULID, then `n`, which counts the Result's `limits` from 1. The CLI accepts any node reference before the `#` (§2) and stores the full id. An owner checkpoint accepts a limit the runner referred (`accepts`); an old owner confirm's `accepts` is replayed as an accept. §8 derives each limit's state, and §8.2 states which move applies from which state. `hyp record` prints the key of each limit and reading it records, under the Result's line, so a later command names them without counting `--limit` or `--reading` flags, and the whole text of each limit it closes (`  closes R-…#n: <text>`). A close covers every case its limit names; a case the Result did not run is a new `--limit` on it, referred when void (ADR 0016). A close records that the case ran, whatever the verdicts: a Result that runs the case and contradicts a Hypothesis closes it too. `hyp record` warns, at exit 0, when its Experiment aims at an open limit that the Result neither closes nor follows with a new `--limit` (`warn: E-… aims at R-…#n and this Result does not close it; …`), since an instrument failure may leave an aimed limit open on purpose.\n84:**One field, one transition.** A payload field that names keyed items names one kind of item and one transition. A Result's `closes` closes limits; an answer's `answers` answers readings, its `accepts` accepts limits and its `returns` returns them; a checkpoint's `refers` refers limits; a Goal add's `lines` writes the Goal's lines; an old checkpoint's `closes`, `revises`, `accepts`, `returns` and `lines` are history; `aims` names intent and settles nothing (ADR 0013). A confirm's `accepts` is history (§8.2).\n86:**[added] lines**: the Goal's lines are the cases every candidate must meet (ADR 0016 amendment \"a Goal line is a limit the owner records\"). They come with the Goal: the owner adds the Goal with `lines` on its `node.add` (`hyp add goal --line TEXT`, repeatable), and no later event adds a line (ADR 0020). A line is never edited: what it means is a reading, a line the Goal no longer needs is referred and accepted, a case under it is a limit the owner returns, and a new line is a new Goal, which may sit in the same store. A line is a limit keyed on the Goal, `<goal-id>#<n>`, where `n` counts the add's `lines` from 1, and it has every move a limit has: a Result `closes` it, an Experiment `aims` at it, a checkpoint `refers` it and an answer `returns` or `accepts` it. Its scope is the Goal, so a Result under any question may close it and an Experiment under any question may aim at it. `hyp add goal` prints the key of each line it writes (`  line G-…#n: <text>`). An old Goal's lines were written on its first owner checkpoint (`hyp checkpoint --role owner --line`, no longer a command); replay still applies them, with the same keys, and that checkpoint counts as the one write. **Cites**: a line names 0 to 10 plain keys (`hyp add goal --cite N=KEY`, repeatable), each settled when the line is written: a line, an accepted or closed limit, an answered reading, or a selected or contradicted Hypothesis (by its id). Anything else is refused at exit 1 (§5 rule 31). A cite carries no relation label: what it means is derived from the key's state at the line's place in the log, a line or an accepted limit **carried**, a closed limit or a contradicted Hypothesis **forbidden**, an answered reading **applied**, a selected Hypothesis **realised**. A Goal's origin is the Goals its cited keys belong to. When a cited key's state later differs, `hyp check` warns (`warn: G-…#n cites H-…, which was realised when the line was written and is unsettled now`) and `hyp show G-…#n` prints both (`cites H-…: realised, now unsettled`); no status changes. A Goal of a store with many Goals is named on a checkpoint with `hyp checkpoint --goal G-…` (without it, exit 2).\n90:**[added] readings**: a Result names a line of the Goal that a verdict rests on and how the run read it (`readings`). A reading's key is `<result-id>#r<n>`, and `n` counts the Result's `readings` from 1. Its states are open and closed, and both are derived. An owner answer closes it: its `answers` names the key, and the item's text is the owner's answer, which `hyp show` prints beside the closed reading (an old owner checkpoint closed it with `closes` and its `summary`). An owner changes an answer they gave with a later answer that names the key again: whether an item is a first answer or a revision is derived from the reading's state, so the old `closes` and `revises` merge (ADR 0020). `hyp answer` warns, at exit 0, when it revises a key that a line of another Goal cites. The answers are kept in merge order, the close first, and the latest is the answer in force; `hyp show` prints it beside the reading and each earlier one beneath, with the Result that applied it. A revision never reopens the reading and changes no event before it: an earlier Result keeps its verdicts, and a Hypothesis it contradicted stays contradicted until a new Result judges a successor. In an old log, a `revises` that sorts before its key's close is refused, and a second `closes` of an answered reading is rejected; two revisions both apply, the later in force. A reading of a retracted Result is left out. It refuses, at exit 1, a key that names no reading, a retracted Result, and an answer under the actor that recorded the Result. An open reading prints under AWAITING OWNER and changes no status: it never changes a question's state or the Goal's. It holds back ledger items on its question only while its **reach** is not empty: the live Hypotheses under that question whose standing the answer can move, read from the Result's own verdicts, which are a candidate the Result judged, and a contradicted rival whose only `contradicts` is that Result's. While the reach is not empty it holds `rivals`, `select` and `choose`, because the answer may revive a rival, drop the lone candidate or change the candidates (§8.1). A reading whose reach is empty, such as one whose Result's Hypotheses another Result has since contradicted, holds nothing. A closed reading is **applied** by the first non-retracted Result that lists its key in `applies`. Each answer is applied by the first non-retracted Result after its checkpoint, and before the next revision, that lists the key in `applies`, and by no other Result: a later Result under the question may have run for another reason, such as a repair, and never read the answer (ADR 0013 amendment of 2026-11-09). A tree written before `applies` existed therefore owes `reading` for answers its Results applied without naming them. The reading is applied when the answer in force is. Until a Result applies it, an answered reading is the runner's `reading` item (§8.1), which holds back the owner's `choose` on its question, because the owner's answer cannot yet have changed the candidates. Closing one does not change the Goal's text. A reading reads either a line of the Goal or an owner's answer to an earlier reading; a reading of an answer names that reading's key in `of`. It narrows the answer and never reopens it: the earlier reading stays closed and its answer stays in force, and the owner changes their words only by a rule change. `of` may name only a closed reading of a non-retracted Result, so a chain always points back in merge order and holds no cycle; a merged log in which the reading of an answer comes before its close rejects that Result. Without this, a verdict's reliance on an unclear Goal line stays in prose the owner has to go looking for. Two readings with the same `of` and the same text, spaces trimmed, **ask the same question**: each holds its own Result's question, and nothing stored ties them. `hyp status` prints the open ones as one AWAITING OWNER line, each key with its question, and `hyp answer` warns (`warn: R-…#r1 asks the same as R-…#r1 and stays open; add --answer R-…#r1`, or `… keeps its earlier answer; add --answer …`) when it leaves a copy behind (ADR 0013).\n92:Content limits, in runes: `title` ≤ 120 and non-empty; `body`, `observation` and `summary` ≤ 600 (an amend's `body` is also non-empty), except a Goal's `body` ≤ 2000, because it holds the owner's met rule; a reading ≤ 600, because it holds both meanings of a Goal line and the question for the owner; `why` 1–300.\n105:4. Wrong parent kind. Goal: parent `\"\"`. A store holds many Goals (ADR 0020): a tree is the view of one Goal and every node whose parents lead to it. DesignQuestion: parent is the Goal or a Hypothesis. Hypothesis: parent is a DesignQuestion. Experiment: parent is a Hypothesis, or a DesignQuestion. Result: parent is an Experiment.\n116:15. A limit key in `closes` or `accepts` that does not name a limit (`bad limit ref`): the key is not `<ref>#<n>` with n ≥ 1; `<ref>` is neither a Result nor the Goal; the Result is retracted; n is greater than the Result's number of limits, or the Goal's number of lines (`bad limit ref: the Goal has N lines`); or the Result is not under the question. For `closes` the question is the one the new Result belongs to (the parent DQ of its Experiment's Hypothesis); for a confirm's `accepts` it is the confirm's DQ. A line is under every question of its own Goal; a close of another Goal's key is refused by rule 32, and an answer's accepts may name keys of several Goals.\n125:24. A `checkpoint` `lines` without the owner role (`bad checkpoint: only an owner writes the Goal's lines`), when the Goal's add carried lines (`bad lines: a Goal's lines come with its node.add, and G-…'s did; a new line is a new Goal`), when an earlier checkpoint wrote lines (`bad lines: the Goal's lines are written once, by <checkpoint-id>; a new line is a new Goal`); the rule is per Goal, so each Goal of a store has its own first checkpoint with lines, with more than 10 lines (`over limit`), or a line outside 1–300 runes.\n132:30. An `answer` that holds a runner field, `summary`, `refers`, `role`, `closes`, `revises` or `lines`, or has a `node` or a `why` (`bad answer: owner and runner fields never mix in one event: <field> is the runner's; …`); with no key in `answers`, `accepts` and `returns` (`bad answer: empty; an answer settles at least one key`); an item text outside 1–600 runes; more than 10 items in a field (`over limit`); a key named twice in `answers` (`bad answers: <key> is named twice`), or a limit key there (`bad answers: <key> is a limit; an owner accepts or returns a limit`). A `checkpoint` that holds `refers` with `role`, `closes`, `revises`, `accepts`, `returns` or `lines` (`bad checkpoint: owner and runner fields never mix in one event: refers is the runner's and <field> the owner's; the owner's acts are an answer`), or that holds neither a non-blank `summary` nor `refers` nor an owner field (`bad checkpoint: empty; a checkpoint holds a summary, refers, or both`). An old owner checkpoint is checked as written: its `summary` is 1–600 runes. A refer's text, when it has one, is 1–600 runes (ADR 0020). A refer with no text in a checkpoint with a blank `summary` (`bad refers: <key> has no reason; a refer says why no test within the Goal's lines can settle it, in its text or the summary`).\n133:31. `lines` on a `node.add` that is not a Goal (`unknown field: lines are for a Goal only`); more than 10 lines or 10 cites on a line (`over limit`); a line outside 1–600 runes; a cite named twice on a line; or a cite not settled when the line is written (`bad cite: line <n> cites <key>, which is not settled: <why>; a cite is a line, an accepted or closed limit, an answered reading, or a selected or contradicted Hypothesis`): a key that names no line, limit or reading, an open limit that no Result closes and no answer accepts, an open reading, a Hypothesis that is neither the current select of its question nor contradicted by a non-retracted Result, or a node of another kind. Each test reads stored facts only. A later change in the cited key's state rejects nothing (§4 lines).\n134:32. A move whose key leads to another Goal (`bad <field>: <key> leads to G-…, not G-…; a move never crosses Goals, and a later Goal takes a case over by citing it`): a `checkpoint` `refers` key whose Goal is not the checkpoint's `node` (`bad refers`); an Experiment `aims` key