+{"parentUuid":"5709ea7f-a18b-41a8-aa53-10c21bf79d36","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ee5b05c9-67f0-483b-8609-eb37b607df8e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XZyWTTDAYZHg4tepmXFkw8","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/core-and-self-review.md:44:| `recipes/recipe-base-arms@1` | a recipe this repository keeps to test hyp: a sonnet runner builds a held-out instrument from the skill-text gate method in its bundle on three hyp arms (template only, a coarse base, fine bases), for `investigations/recipe-base`; copied by hand from a version of `recipe-authoring-arms` (its `recipe.md` names it by hash), because `--from` copies the same name only: the one bootstrap exception to starting from a base, had a base shipped (ADR 0009) | public |\ndocs/core-and-self-review.md:62:| arm (id-citation-arms) | an Arm: a hyp binary built in Launch from main or main plus one rival's patch, named by sha256, the subject the cases run (version 1 read binaries built by hand from absolute paths) |\ndocs/replay-spec.md:256:  A pin to a bundle without an Evidence or Standalone command, such as a known-answer fixture, is held by its hash alone (§7.3). The CLI derives the mark from files when it reads, as it does `recipeHashes`. Replay never reads files, and the mark never changes a status. `hyp record` warns, and `hyp show`, `hyp status`, `hyp render` and `hyp check` show it. Nothing is refused. Limits: a script named only in prose, whose output a Result cites, is not seen; neither is a hand-made output file cited beside a real command record, or a script with none of the marks in reason 1. A hand-written `command.json` that claims a clean run with passing known answers satisfies reason 2; a reviewer checks the record's `command.log` and `known-answers.log`. A script that only a Launch, Doctor, Drive or Cleanup command reaches, cited from another Experiment, is held by its hash alone: hyp runs it in a sandbox, but no reason ties that sandbox's record to the Result. The gate proves that a bundle reproduces its own expected outputs, not that those outputs are right: a weak or wrong case needs review (ADR 0002). A case and a run get the same environment, working directory and hyp, and a case compares exit code, stdout and the tree under `$HYP_OUT` (ADR 0003), so a passed gate carries over to the run, except for what a case cannot see: a read of a fixed absolute path, a read from the network, the versions of the tools on the host `PATH` after hyp, and a write outside `$HYP_OUT`; all pass the double run. The paths of a run's ARGS are the subject's, and a limit of the host, such as the length of a Unix socket path, can differ between machines. Reason 2 names why a cited record of the pin is not clean: the first failed command, or known answers that did not pass.\ndocs/sandbox-spec.md:30:Evidence that the operator gathers by hand while the sandbox exists is not a command, and is unchanged: it is the files saved under `.tree/evidence/<E-id>/`. Scripted Evidence is the same step done by a command, which is why it runs after Cleanup: it reads only the kept files, so it can be run again.\ndocs/sandbox-spec.md:55:A sandbox's state is derived from stored facts, as a tree's statuses are, and is never stored as a word: each recipe's `setup`, the lock and the record's `removing` field. Each command refuses by state, as this table says; a cell that says \"runs\" runs as §1 says. In code, `deriveState` in `internal/sandbox/state.go` is the rule, and `TestStateTable` mirrors the table by hand, row for row.\nskills/hyp-verification/RECIPE-FORMAT.md:845:4. A hand-written `command.json` that claims a clean run with passing\nskills/hyp-verification/SKILL.md:79:Evidence comes in two kinds. Evidence gathered by hand is what you save\nskills/hyp-verification/SKILL.md:94:Store what you observed by hand under `.tree/evidence/<E-id>/`, with short file names:\nskills/hyp-goal-run/SKILL.md:3:description: \"Run a hyp tree under an owner's Goal and met rule as an unattended discovery /goal: act only on what OWED says the runner owes, test before asking, write readings the owner can answer, reassess the targets under the owner's answers, and stop with a report the owner can read from hyp status. Use when a /goal prompt names this skill.\"\n29:- **The Goal's lines are the owner's.** The owner writes the cases every candidate must meet once, as the Goal's lines. A line is owed on each question until a Result that ran its case covers every candidate there, and `hyp status` shows each line as covered or uncovered.\n39:| **Owner** | Writes the Goal's lines once. Answers what `hyp status` lists under AWAITING OWNER: what a line means, whether a referred case runs, which of the candidates that meet every line to choose. | Runs a case, types a verdict or edits what was recorded |\n57:hyp's own design is decided and built this way. Both trees below are in this repository; run `hyp status` in their directory to see the same output.\n61:The tree `investigations/id-citation` settled which rule stops a write command from appending text that cites an id that is no node. Its one Result, R-BV4CWV, supported three rivals and contradicted the fourth, and the owner selected among the three as a trade-off. `hyp status` in that directory:\n102:`investigations/checkpoint-store-design` recorded the design of the shared store as a discovery Goal, four DesignQuestions the owner settled in a session. `investigations/checkpoint-store` then built it as a build Goal, G-D1CH1R, whose candidate is a hyp commit. Its owner wrote nine lines, eight of them marked `[P]` or `[N]`. `hyp status` there (lines shortened):\n142:If `hyp status` says there is no .tree store, run `hyp init` at the repository root.\n148:Show me `hyp status` and stop.\n158:Your answers later are `hyp answer` events under the same name, for example `hyp answer --answer \"R-…#r1=<your words>\"`.\n160:From there the [hyp-prototype skill](skills/hyp-prototype/SKILL.md) guides the next rounds: experiments, verdicts, selects and checks. When `hyp status` lists something under AWAITING OWNER, it is yours to answer. [example.sh](skills/hyp-prototype/example.sh) runs a complete investigation in a throwaway directory.\n167:2. **`/goal <that prompt>`** runs the Goal with the `hyp-goal-run` skill. The run does only what OWED says it owes, tests before it asks, and stops with a report you can read from `hyp status`.\nskills/hyp-owner-grill/SKILL.md:34:| what a run may change, may only read, or must never do | the Goal body's `May change:`, `Read only:` and `Never:` headings | yes |\nskills/hyp-owner-grill/SKILL.md:96:The Goal body ends with the run's constraints, one heading per line, so a run and a prompt generator can find them:\nskills/hyp-owner-grill/SKILL.md:99:May change: <the repository, how it is changed (for example commits on one local branch, a PR never merged), and the texts the change makes wrong>\nskills/hyp-owner-grill/SKILL.md:100:Read only: <repositories, directories or data the run may read but never change>\nskills/hyp-owner-grill/SKILL.md:108:Write the `/goal` prompt for hyp-goal-run, under 4000 characters: the tree, the runner's actor, the Goal id, the stop clauses, and the Goal body's `May change:`, `Read only:` and `Never:` lines copied as they are. Put general practice in the skill, not the prompt. For a build Goal it also says:\nskills/hyp-goal-run/SKILL.md:12:- The Goal's body states the run's constraints under three headings: `May change:` (what you may write, and how), `Read only:` (what you may read but never change) and `Never:` (acts you never take). Obey them. A `/goal` prompt may narrow them, never widen them. If a step needs something they forbid, refer it with a reason or record a reading, and stop.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e57c533a-9ade-4ba9-8a4a-37b17c97bf8a","timestamp":"2026-11-12T14:22:36.132Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"docs/core-and-self-review.md:44:| `recipes/recipe-base-arms@1` | a recipe this reposi