he owner writes the cases every candidate must meet once, as the Goal's lines. A line is owed on each question until a Result that ran its case covers every candidate there, and `hyp status` shows each line as covered or uncovered.\n39:| **Owner** | Writes the Goal's lines once. Answers what `hyp status` lists under AWAITING OWNER: what a line means, whether a referred case runs, which of the candidates that meet every line to choose. | Runs a case, types a verdict or edits what was recorded |\n57:hyp's own design is decided and built this way. Both trees below are in this repository; run `hyp status` in their directory to see the same output.\n61:The tree `investigations/id-citation` settled which rule stops a write command from appending text that cites an id that is no node. Its one Result, R-BV4CWV, supported three rivals and contradicted the fourth, and the owner selected among the three as a trade-off. `hyp status` in that directory:\n102:`investigations/checkpoint-store-design` recorded the design of the shared store as a discovery Goal, four DesignQuestions the owner settled in a session. `investigations/checkpoint-store` then built it as a build Goal, G-D1CH1R, whose candidate is a hyp commit. Its owner wrote nine lines, eight of them marked `[P]` or `[N]`. `hyp status` there (lines shortened):\n142:If `hyp status` says there is no .tree store, run `hyp init` at the repository root.\n148:Show me `hyp status` and stop.\n158:Your answers later are `hyp answer` events under the same name, for example `hyp answer --answer \"R-…#r1=<your words>\"`.\n160:From there the [hyp-prototype skill](skills/hyp-prototype/SKILL.md) guides the next rounds: experiments, verdicts, selects and checks. When `hyp status` lists something under AWAITING OWNER, it is yours to answer. [example.sh](skills/hyp-prototype/example.sh) runs a complete investigation in a throwaway directory.\n167:2. **`/goal <that prompt>`** runs the Goal with the `hyp-goal-run` skill. The run does only what OWED says it owes, tests before it asks, and stops with a report you can read from `hyp status`.\nskills/hyp-owner-grill/SKILL.md:34:| what a run may change, may only read, or must never do | the Goal body's `May change:`, `Read only:` and `Never:` headings | yes |\nskills/hyp-owner-grill/SKILL.md:96:The Goal body ends with the run's constraints, one heading per line, so a run and a prompt generator can find them:\nskills/hyp-owner-grill/SKILL.md:99:May change: <the repository, how it is changed (for example commits on one local branch, a PR never merged), and the texts the change makes wrong>\nskills/hyp-owner-grill/SKILL.md:100:Read only: <repositories, directories or data the run may read but never change>\nskills/hyp-owner-grill/SKILL.md:108:Write the `/goal` prompt for hyp-goal-run, under 4000 characters: the tree, the runner's actor, the Goal id, the stop clauses, and the Goal body's `May change:`, `Read only:` and `Never:` lines copied as they are. Put general practice in the skill, not the prompt. For a build Goal it also says:\nskills/hyp-goal-run/SKILL.md:12:- The Goal's body states the run's constraints under three headings: `May change:` (what you may write, and how), `Read only:` (what you may read but never change) and `Never:` (acts you never take). Obey them. A `/goal` prompt may narrow them, never widen them. If a step needs something they forbid, refer it with a reason or record a reading, and stop.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sandboxViolations":[],"sandboxed":true,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5709ea7f-a18b-41a8-aa53-10c21bf79d36","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"rule","ruleSource":"cliArg"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-cli","cwd":"/Users/datguy/.hyp-sandbox/g146-base-a-2/wt","sessionId":"e3ce0dbb-c168-4a49-9caf-ecc01cb40fbf","version":"2.1.322","gitBranch":"work"}
