 Result keeps its verdicts, and a Hypothesis it contradicted stays contradicted until a new Result judges a successor. In an old log, a `revises` that sorts before its key's close is refused, and a second `closes` of an answered reading is rejected; two revisions both apply, the later in force. A reading of a retracted Result is left out. It refuses, at exit 1, a key that names no reading, a retracted Result, and an answer under the actor that recorded the Result. An open reading prints under AWAITING OWNER and changes no status: it never changes a question's state or the Goal's. It holds back ledger items on its question only while its **reach** is not empty: the live Hypotheses under that question whose standing the answer can move, read from the Result's own verdicts, which are a candidate the Result judged, and a contradicted rival whose only `contradicts` is that Result's. While the reach is not empty it holds `rivals`, `select` and `choose`, because the answer may revive a rival, drop the lone candidate or change the candidates (§8.1). A reading whose reach is empty, such as one whose Result's Hypotheses another Result has since contradicted, holds nothing. A closed reading is **applied** by the first non-retracted Result that lists its key in `applies`. Each answer is applied by the first non-retracted Result after its checkpoint, and before the next revision, that lists the key in `applies`, and by no other Result: a later Result under the question may have run for another reason, such as a repair, and never read the answer (ADR 0013 amendment of 2026-11-09). A tree written before `applies` existed therefore owes `reading` for answers its Results applied without naming them. The reading is applied when the answer in force is. Until a Result applies it, an answered reading is the runner's `reading` item (§8.1), which holds back the owner's `choose` on its question, because the owner's answer cannot yet have changed the candidates. Closing one does not change the Goal's text. A reading reads either a line of the Goal or an owner's answer to an earlier reading; a reading of an answer names that reading's key in `of`. It narrows the answer and never reopens it: the earlier reading stays closed and its answer stays in force, and the owner changes their words only by a rule change. `of` may name only a closed reading of a non-retracted Result, so a chain always points back in merge order and holds no cycle; a merged log in which the reading of an answer comes before its close rejects that Result. Without this, a verdict's reliance on an unclear Goal line stays in prose the owner has to go looking for. Two readings with the same `of` and the same text, spaces trimmed, **ask the same question**: each holds its own Result's question, and nothing stored ties them. `hyp status` prints the open ones as one AWAITING OWNER line, each key with its question, and `hyp answer` warns (`warn: R-…#r1 asks the same as R-…#r1 and stays open; add --answer R-…#r1`, or `… keeps its earlier answer; add --answer …`) when it leaves a copy behind (ADR 0013).\n143:- `hyp select Q H` is admitted only when the ledger (§8.1) owes it: a `select` item for H, or the owner's `choose` on Q with H a candidate. Otherwise it is refused, naming the first thing Q's turn waits on (§8.1), in this order: the first item the runner owes on Q (`Q owes the runner first: limit R-…#1`), an open reading on Q that waits for the owner (`open reading R-…#r1 waits for the owner`), a referred limit (`referred limit R-…#n waits for the owner: …`), the premise (`H-… assumes H-…, in Q-… open; decide Q-… first`), the sub-question (`awaiting_subquestions: H-… has a sub-question not answered`); or else Q's state. When a select is owed for another Hypothesis or H is not a candidate, the refusal says so. An answered Q owes no select: `hyp unselect` first, then select. The select waits on an open limit under Q whichever Hypotheses its Result judged, since its case may apply to every candidate (§8.1, ADR 0016). `hyp show Q` lists, under `not separated:`, each pair of live rivals no non-retracted Result gives different verdicts (`replay.Projection.Unseparated`); it is a derived list, not a signal, and changes nothing the ledger owes. The open-reading refusal names the reach: `open reading R-…#r1 waits for the owner; its answer can move H-…`. `hyp select Q` with no Hypothesis prints what the same ledger reads: `suggested: H-…` for an owed select, `trade-off: owner decides between …` for an owed choice, or `suggested: none (…)` with the refusal's reason. `replay.Projection.AdmitSelect` holds the rule. Replay still applies a select in a merged log.\n217:- A **candidate** is a live child Hypothesis whose status is `supported`; neither stale nor an open sub-question removes it. Two or more candidates are a **trade-off**, a state derived from the candidates and never recorded; an older select's stored `tradeoff` is ignored. The owner ends a trade-off with their choice, recorded as the select (ADR 0015): hyp admits that select only when the ledger owes the choice (§5.1, §8.1). The select holds what was chosen, not who chose it: its actor is whoever wrote it, and no status reads its `why`.\n225:A change that adds no candidate, such as a retract that leaves the selected Hypothesis supported, a new rival or an amend, leaves the question answered: the select is the gate, and the ledger owes what such a change brings (§8.1). The verifier-loop signal and its chain were removed with the verifier role (ADR 0015).\n229:**Suggestion:** `hyp select Q` with no Hypothesis reads the ledger, as admission does (§5.1): it suggests the Hypothesis of an owed `select`, prints \"trade-off: owner decides between …\" for an owed `choose`, and otherwise prints `suggested: none (…)` with the reason a select would be refused, such as `awaiting_subquestions: H-… has a sub-question not answered`.\n233:- `complete` when the Goal has at least one live DQ as a child and every live DQ whose parent is the Goal is `answered`; a Goal with no live top-level DQ is `in_progress`, because nothing has been asked yet. It reads no line: a line of the Goal is owed on each top-level question as a `limit` item (§8.1) and the select waits on it, and the owner's \"met\" is a complete Goal with no line under OWED. hyp stores no word for it. Sub-questions are covered through the `answered` rule of §7.4, so sub-questions under rejected rival hypotheses never block the Goal. (This clarifies the advice's \"nothing stale\" clause: stale on the selected path already prevents `answered`.)\n343:- otherwise `open`. An open limit whose key a checkpoint lists in `refers` is **referred**: the runner says no test within the Goal can close it, or that its case is void for every candidate, and only the owner can settle it: by accepting it, so this tree does not run the case, or by returning it (`returns`), because the Goal covers its case. The latest `refers` or `returns` in merge order names whose turn an open limit is: a returned limit is the runner's again, owed, with the select waiting on it like any open limit, until a Result closes it or a later checkpoint refers it again. The select and the choice on its question wait on a referred limit too, because a return sends its case back to run and its Result can change the candidates: `hyp select` refuses with `referred limit R-…#n waits for the owner: an accept releases the select, a return sends its case to run`. New rivals do not wait on it. A return also reopens a limit an owner checkpoint accepted, so an owner who changes their mind writes one checkpoint: the latest of accept and return stands. A limit's category is never stored (ADR 0016): a close with the closer's verdicts, an accept, and the latest refer or return are what it is.\n345:Lines **[added]**: a line of the Goal (§4 lines) is a limit with the Goal for its Result. It has a hand and closers as a limit does, and is neither open nor closed: whether its case ran is per candidate, its coverage. `Open()` of a line means only that its hand is not an accept. **Coverage**: a Result covers a Hypothesis when it has a verdict on it, or when one of the Result's run subjects holds the Hypothesis's whole subject (§4 subject; `Projection.Covers`). Coverage says the bytes ran, not that the claim held. A line is **covered** on a question when a closer of the line covers each of its candidates, and **uncovered** there otherwise. An unaccepted, unreferred line is owed on each live top-level question where it is uncovered (`Projection.Uncovered`), so a new candidate is owed the line again. Coverage is not a state of the move table: it changes when a candidate is added or falls, with no move on the key, and no move reads it. `hyp show G-…#n` prints the line's text, its hand when it has one (`hand: accepted by o`), each cite with what it meant and, when that differs, what it would mean now (`cites H-…: realised, now unsettled`), each top-level question's candidates with `covered` or `uncovered: H-…`, each closer with what it ran on (`ran in R-…: ran on H-… (alias): supports; ran on H-…`), the aiming Experiments and the history. A verdict is per Hypothesis, never per line: a Result that closes several lines and contradicts H says the cases ran and H failed, not which line, so nothing prints `fails <line>`. `hyp status` prints a LINES section under the GOAL line, one line each with its facts joined by `; `, each only when it has one: its hand, `covered on Q-…, Q-…` and `uncovered on Q-…: H-…` for each live top-level question with a candidate, and `ran in R-…`; a line with none prints `no candidate`. Neither `open` nor `closed` prints for a line. The section also prints and the mark `(no one closer covers H-… and H-… together)` when the Hypotheses selected on two or more top-level questions were never covered by one closer (`Projection.NotTogether`); a claim that depends on its host is covered only together with it. No item and no status reads the 